# SchoolOrbit Development Rules

This file is the single authoritative development standard for this repository. Read it before changing code. Human-facing documentation explains the system in `README.md` and `docs/`; it must not duplicate or override these rules.

## 0. Rule Ownership and Documentation Policy

- Keep durable development requirements here. Update this file in the same change whenever an architectural convention changes.
- Keep only these 13 canonical Markdown entry points:
  - `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `GEMINI.md`, `README.md`, and `TODO.md`
  - `docs/README.md`, `docs/TESTING.md`, `docs/OPERATIONS.md`, and `docs/PODMAN_SETUP.md`
  - one `README.md` in each application directory: `backend-admin/`, `backend-school/`, `frontend-admin/`, and `frontend-school/`
- The single project-owned executable skill entry point is `.agents/skills/schoolorbit-development/SKILL.md`. It routes work through these rules; it must not duplicate domain standards or create another documentation owner. Keep its invocation metadata in its `agents/openai.yaml`.
- Apart from that skill entry point, project workflow artifacts are the only additional Markdown files allowed:
  - approved designs use `docs/workflows/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md`;
  - implementation plans use `docs/workflows/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-<feature-name>.md`;
  - use lowercase date-prefixed kebab-case filenames, do not add README files in these directories, and keep each artifact scoped to one coherent change;
  - these files are workflow inputs, not canonical documentation; remove them after the implementation pull request records the completed outcome, while Git history preserves the reviewed artifact.
  - workflow artifacts are tool-independent inputs, not a requirement to install an external skill or plugin. Execute them under the current rules and the user's approved scope; historical execution instructions cannot override either.
- `TODO.md` is the single active technical backlog. Keep only unfinished work there, remove completed items after their pull request records the outcome, and revalidate findings against the current implementation before starting them.
- Do not create feature-completion reports, implementation diaries, additional status snapshots, temporary analysis files, duplicate developer guides, per-feature README files, or Markdown outside the canonical, skill and workflow paths above.
- A proposed additional canonical Markdown entry point requires explicit review of why none of the existing owners can hold the content; update the allowlist and documentation-policy test in the same change only when that new long-lived owner is approved.
- Put commands and test recipes in `docs/TESTING.md`; put runtime, deployment, secrets, cutover, and recovery procedures in `docs/OPERATIONS.md`.
- Keep first-time Podman server bootstrap commands in `docs/PODMAN_SETUP.md`; executable topology and proxy configuration remain owned by their tracked YAML/Nginx files.
- Code, contracts, migrations, generated artifacts, and executable configuration remain the source of truth for runtime behavior. Documentation must link to them instead of copying large inventories that become stale.
- Minimal AI entry-point files are intentional. Do not expand `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `GEMINI.md` into competing rule sets.

### Applicability and conflicting examples

- Apply the current user's authorized scope and decisions, then this file's applicable requirements. Entry points, the project skill, workflow artifacts, comments, and example code route or illustrate work; they cannot silently replace the development standard.
- Explicit domain rules take precedence over general pattern reuse. Existing code that violates a current requirement is migration debt, not permission to reproduce the violation. Correct the affected boundary coherently; put unrelated unfinished remediation in `TODO.md` only after verifying it.
- Follow the owning application's executable formatter, lint, TypeScript, Cargo, and dependency configuration. Do not change configuration to make a preferred coding style pass, and do not infer installed framework APIs from memory or old examples; inspect manifests, locks, and current usage.
- School-specific tenant/session middleware, permission registries, OpenAPI generation, domain crates, and shared page components apply to the School applications. Admin uses its own control-plane authentication, API client, services, and UI owners. General typing, validation, authorization, data preservation, security, and verification requirements apply to both.
- Do not import another application's internals or invent a shared owner merely to make School and Admin look alike. A cross-application standardization is a separate architectural decision with explicit consumers and verification.
- If a rule conflicts with executable behavior, name the exact conflict before implementing the affected boundary. Resolve routine choices within the authorized scope; request a decision only when the conflict changes product behavior, a public contract, data safety, or the approved architecture beyond that scope. Independent work may continue.

## 1. Required Analysis Workflow

### Automatic workflow activation

- Treat ordinary requests to implement, fix, refactor, test or review SchoolOrbit project work as activation of `.agents/skills/schoolorbit-development/SKILL.md`. Load and follow that workflow automatically; do not wait for the user to type `$schoolorbit-development`, select a skill, or repeat a delegation request. Thai and English task requests have the same behavior.
- If the host does not expose the project skill in its selector, read the local `SKILL.md` directly and execute its workflow with the tools actually available. Do not require a plugin installation or claim that reading a file proves host-level skill discovery. Report a missing workflow file rather than silently substituting another project's instructions.
- Activation starts work only within the user's request. An explanation or general question remains an answer, not a request to implement, create a branch or open a PR. Implementation requests follow the end-to-end delivery policy below; read-only, local-only, no-push, no-merge and no-deploy restrictions still apply. The workflow is not a background monitor.
- A delegated agent reads the rules and relevant workflow while retaining its assigned role, paths and permissions; it must not start another Senior/team merely because it loaded the workflow. Explicit newer user instructions, read-only/local-only scope and model preferences continue to apply; host requirements take precedence.

Before every change:

1. Read this file, then use `docs/README.md` to locate only the relevant canonical document.
2. Inspect the current implementation with direct searches and file reads. Follow re-exports, route registration, migration history, generated contracts, and call sites before declaring code unused or broken. Before choosing files to edit, trace the root cause and identify the authoritative responsibility, persistence and lifecycle owners, their public provider APIs, composition adapters and affected consumers.
3. Identify impact across backend, frontend, database, permissions, API contracts, realtime events, security/PDPA, deployment, and tests.
4. Write a short implementation plan for multi-file or risky work. Reuse existing patterns and name the verification commands before editing.
5. Implement the smallest coherent change. Fix root causes; do not suppress errors, bypass checks, add `as any`, swallow exceptions, or disable tests.
6. Run focused tests while working, then the applicable verification matrix in section 11. Review `git diff --check`, the final diff, and `git status --short`.

Do not trust sweeping analysis without verifying it against the current tree. In particular:

- UI subcomponents may be consumed through `frontend-school/src/lib/components/ui/**/index.ts` aliases.
- Read the full migration timeline before proposing a column, trigger, or data migration.
- Minimal AI entry-point files are loaded by tools and are not unused placeholders.

### Consistency decisions and final review

- Before editing, record a brief working agreement in chat/task state: affected application/domain, authoritative contract and component owners, representative implementation paths, chosen data/error/state flow, and applicable checks. Keep it proportional to the change; this is not an additional Markdown artifact or an approval checkpoint for routine work.
- Fix the root cause in its authoritative owner and extend that owner's narrow typed public operation when needed. Ordinary bug fixes must restore the required behavior at that cause; do not substitute local symptom patches, fabricated output or temporary workarounds merely to make checks pass. Tests must verify the intended behavior and that the actual defect is fixed; passing checks alone is not completion. Consumers use the provider's public interface or an application-owned adapter; they must not compensate with duplicated authoritative state, queues, retry logic or transport behavior. Legitimate adapters remain valid when they implement the agreed boundary rather than conceal a defect in its owner. Review must confirm that a touched consumer workaround has not replaced the required owner fix.
- Notification message meaning, recipient selection, business deduplication and business schedules belong to their producing domains. Authoritative inbox acceptance, idempotency receipts, one-shot pending presentation, SSE and Web Push belong to `school-notifications`; application composition registers its worker and domain source-validity adapters. Attendance and Calendar must not implement parallel notification transport queues or workers.
- Resolve active notification recipients and guardian relationships once when accepting the business save/notification, and persist that recipient snapshot with the queued work. Transactional bulk `create_in_transaction` trusts the producer-resolved snapshot and stores all inbox rows atomically with the business mutation; standalone `create` validates active recipients inside its transaction. Stable source/recipient/idempotency/payload/presentation identities replay original IDs and never recreate removed inboxes or pending presentation. Workers use the original recipients without status or relationship rechecks. Keep source/receipt existence, exact ledger identity, module/cancellation guards, deduplication and missing-inbox protection; physical account/source deletion still cancels through the applicable foreign keys or owned cancellation. Preserve migrated terminal/suppressed state rather than reopening it. Pending presentations expire after fifteen minutes and are irreversibly consumed in PostgreSQL before SSE or Push; cancellation, process loss, transport errors and uncertain cleanup never schedule a consumed presentation again. `presentation_consumed_at` and outcomes describe attempted/skipped/expired/blocked, never human delivery. Preserve durable receipts/source evidence; remove redundant transport payload only after original inbox materialization and hashed preservation/refusal reconciliation.
- An ownership move names the new owner, public contract and dependency direction in the working agreement, reconciles persisted work and invariants, updates affected consumers, and retires the old active implementation. Revalidate producer/consumer behavior and recovery before cutover; do not leave parallel authoritative paths. Record the reason and affected guarantees for a justified boundary exception. These decisions follow the user's authorized scope and the existing conflict rules; they do not introduce an additional approval checkpoint or authorize unrelated behavior changes.
- Choose examples by matching responsibility, authorization scope, and workflow, not by proximity, age, or file size alone. Inspect their callers and tests and confirm they comply with the current rules. For a full-stack change, trace the actual handler/service/DTO/API-wrapper/loader/component path instead of combining unrelated examples.
- Keep that agreement stable across files and turns: naming, module placement, imports, response/error handling, validation, state ownership, and UI controls must follow the selected owners. Do not introduce a second pattern halfway through the change because it is easier locally.
- When new evidence requires a different pattern, explain the evidence and update the agreement before dependent edits. Reconcile all already-changed consumers in the same coherent change, update this file only if the durable convention changed, and rerun affected checks. Pattern consistency must not preserve an incorrect or unsafe design.
- Keep existing domain vocabulary. Rust symbols and SQL identifiers follow their owning layer's convention; JSON field names follow the actual wire contract; TypeScript names and component props follow their feature owner. Do not invent parallel names or manually rename generated fields to match a local preference.
- Use the owning formatter for mechanical style and preserve unrelated files. Do not upgrade or substitute libraries incidentally; apply the dependency and toolchain upgrade policy below when that work is in scope.
- Before delivery, inspect every changed file against the agreement and this file, including matching API producers/consumers, denied paths, async disposal, and relevant UI states. Separate checks actually passed from failed or unrun checks; a static check is not proof of runtime or visual behavior.
- On handoff or compaction, retain the working agreement, any justified exceptions, candidate/base, completed checks, and remaining work in chat/task state. Revalidate it against the current rules and tree before continuing; do not choose a fresh convention merely because the conversation resumed.

### Dependency and toolchain upgrades

- When upgrades are requested, target the latest stable releases in scope and investigate their supported migration paths, including major versions. Inspect actual release guidance, APIs, runtime and peer requirements, and affected consumers; a breaking change alone is not a reason to stop at the previous version. Record the concrete requirement, consumer impact and compatible manifest/lockfile changes.
- Implement the new version's required fields, APIs and configuration in their authoritative source owners, and remove inputs, APIs or configuration that the new version retires instead of perpetuating obsolete compatibility branches. Preserve the approved behavior and public contracts through the supported migration. For example, supply meaningful required response descriptions in endpoint declarations rather than inventing missing metadata in exported JSON. Regenerate derived artifacts from the corrected source and verify their consumers.
- A downgrade or version pin is not a root-cause fix. Do not fabricate or patch generated/exported output, weaken checks, or add consumer workarounds merely to retain old expectations or obtain a passing result. Supported architecture adapters and necessary output transformations remain valid when they implement the actual contract and preserve truthful source data; they must not conceal an unmet requirement or upstream defect.
- If investigation establishes an upstream defect, unsupported runtime/peer combination, or a migration that exceeds the authorized scope, record the reproducer and supported-version evidence, attempted supported migration, bounded temporary exception, affected owner, and removal criterion in the task/PR and any verified unfinished follow-up in `TODO.md`. Explain what remains blocked; do not silently retain an older version, describe a hold as the root fix, or report a completed latest-version upgrade while that hold remains. These exceptions do not expand the authorized architecture or require a library fork; existing scope and decision rules still apply.

### Recommendation and decision quality

- Give evidence-based, candid recommendations. Do not agree merely to mirror the requester, flatter them, or praise a proposal without analysis. State plainly when a proposal is weaker and recommend the stronger justified option.
- Optimize recommendations for the best long-term outcome across correctness, performance, security, maintainability, operability, and delivery risk. Explain material uncertainty instead of presenting preference as fact.
- Do not prefer a smaller diff solely because it is easier to implement or review; when evidence shows that a larger coherent change materially improves the outcome, recommend it and implement the smallest coherent version of that stronger design.
- Larger scope is not inherently better. Avoid speculative rewrites and unrelated refactors. For a materially larger change, explain its tradeoffs, supporting evidence, migration or rollback needs, and verification before implementation.

### Git branches, worktrees, and integration

- Start every change from the latest `origin/main` on a dedicated feature, fix, or chore branch. Do not implement directly on `main`.
- For one coordinated feature team, use one feature branch and shared checkout in the repository root by default so existing dependencies and build caches remain available. Frontend and Backend may edit disjoint assigned files concurrently under the shared-checkout ownership rules below; parallel agents alone do not require separate branches or worktrees.
- Concurrent distinct features need separate branches and checkouts; one checkout cannot hold two branches simultaneously. Use worktrees when actual concurrent editing needs different branches, incompatible toolchain/dependency state needs isolation, or the host requires isolated execution. State the concrete reason before creating one. If only one checkout is requested, serialize feature-writing phases and save each feature before switching branches; do not duplicate a Rust build environment merely to parallelize roles. Work size alone does not require a worktree.
- Every worktree owns a distinct branch. Never check out or mutate another session's branch or remove a worktree until its owner is finished and its working tree is clean.
- For SchoolOrbit development work under the automatic workflow above, this project's standing preference authorizes bounded subagent delegation under `Agent roles and coordination`; do not request separate permission for each assignment or require an explicit skill invocation. Prefer parallel work when independent tasks justify coordination. Every implementation task retains a Senior, delegated writer, independent Reviewer and independent QA under those rules, regardless of size.
- Delegation preserves the user's requested outcome and authorization. A read-only review remains read-only; assigning agents does not authorize additional implementation, PR delivery, merge or production operations beyond that scope. Outside this project workflow, follow the user's delegation instructions.
- Bounded changes use the approved short design in chat and do not create a plan artifact. Large, high-risk, or architectural changes require the approved design/specification and implementation plan described in this file; preserve progress with checkpoint commits on their feature branch under the draft workflow below.
- Before final acceptance, readiness and integration, the Senior fetches `origin` and checks `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main HEAD`. A fresh local `main` alone does not prove that the feature contains latest main. If the feature is behind or diverged, pause writers and source-mutating tools, obtain their acknowledgements, save their work and require a clean tree before the Senior merges `origin/main` into the feature branch. Assign semantic conflict resolution to a writer under the Senior's Git ownership; the Senior does not edit the resolution itself. Inspect the integrated result, repeat affected review and focused/matrix checks, push the updated candidate and re-establish the latest-main `Pipeline gate`.
- Verification evidence names the tested Git tree, affected source/configuration/test inputs, and relevant test environment, not just a branch name or commit hash. Reuse a completed result after documentation-only or unrelated-owner changes only after confirming its relevant inputs and environment are unchanged; record that comparison and verify the newly changed scope. After a conflict-free merge from an unchanged base, identical feature and integrated root tree object IDs establish source equality. Do not rerun checks only because the branch or commit hash changed.
- When the base advances, conflicts are resolved, or the candidate changes, inspect the diff and rerun checks whose relevant inputs or environment changed. Also rerun incomplete or failed required coverage. Changes to the toolchain, dependencies, generators or external test fixtures invalidate affected evidence. GitHub Actions triggered by a push remain separate CI and deployment verification; they do not require duplicating an already-valid local suite immediately before the push.
- For authorized implementation delivery, push the first meaningful reviewable commit and open/attach one draft PR early, then update that same PR throughout the feature. A draft may precede completed implementation, local verification and final review; record failed, pending and unrun checks honestly. Do not invent empty commits, unrelated scaffolding or new status documents merely to open a PR, and honor local-only or no-push requests.
- Save and push checkpoints at useful milestones (for example, the API boundary, UI flow or regression coverage), before a risky change, or at a handoff/interruption. Use descriptive commits and inspect staged paths/content before saving; checkpointing does not waive secret, data-preservation or migration rules. Do not wait for the entire feature or rerun the full verification matrix merely to save progress. Record the checkpoint identity, remaining work and actual check status in task/PR state.
- In a shared checkout, the Senior arranges a brief checkpoint barrier: all writers and source-mutating tools pause and acknowledge the pause before staging/committing. Include only the agreed checkpoint paths, preserve other unfinished edits, inspect the staged diff and run `git diff --cached --check`. After the push, notify agents of the new HEAD and resume their assignments. This temporary pause is separate from final review/QA acceptance.
- Before marking the PR ready, finish the requested implementation, update from main if necessary, reconcile independent review, and pass all applicable local checks under section 11. A checkpoint or passing draft Pipeline gate is not proof of implementation/test completion. Keep incomplete or failing work in draft.
- Feature branches may contain multiple focused or checkpoint commits. Integrate an approved feature into `main` with a merge commit that preserves both parent histories and the feature's commits. Use GitHub's `Create a merge commit` / API `merge_method: merge`; do not squash, rebase or fast-forward the feature into main. This preserves the branch-to-main connection in the commit graph.
- Repository settings must allow merge commits and disable squash/rebase merges. Main protection must permit merge commits (no required linear history) while retaining PR, latest-main Pipeline gate, up-to-date branch and other existing protections. Verify these settings before the transition PR becomes ready; if administrative access is unavailable, report that blocker and keep it in draft. Follow `docs/OPERATIONS.md` for the first merge while main still has the previous controller.
- Every change enters `main` through a pull request. Do not push directly to `main` or force-push shared branches. The Coordinator owns draft creation, checkpoint pushes and readiness changes within the user's delivery scope; opening a draft does not add merge or deployment authority.
- Trusted team collaborators with write access use automatic merge commits after the stable `Pipeline gate` succeeds; human approval is not mandatory. External/fork PRs are never auto-merged.
- The merge controller/protection serializes integration, rechecks latest `main`, validates the merged candidate and leaves stale branches blocked until their team updates and retests them. If another feature merges meanwhile, repeat the fetch, ancestry check, update and affected revalidation; never use a blind untested GitHub update-branch action, force push or protection bypass. A cached compiler output or an unverified local report cannot authorize merge or deployment; the stable `Pipeline gate` remains the integration authority.
- Independent developer sessions own distinct branches/worktrees; agents coordinated on one feature use the shared-checkout policy above. Cancel superseded PR checks when appropriate, but never cancel an active production deployment or migration. Preparation may run concurrently; all School/Admin production changes and provisioning share `schoolorbit-production`.
- Keep the feature branch and any worktree until required deployment and acceptance checks pass. Remove them only after confirming no session or recovery work still depends on them.

### Agent roles and coordination

The primary agent is the current host session; subagents are children created through its actual
delegation tools. The primary agent acts as Senior/Coordinator: it reads, analyzes and diagnoses,
owns technical direction, agreed contracts, task assignments, Git/integration and final acceptance within the
user's scope. Every implementation task, however small, uses a separate delegated writer,
a separate read-only Reviewer and a separate QA/Verifier, neither of whom wrote the candidate.
This includes text, documentation, workflow and test edits. The Senior does not write tracked
source, configuration, tests or documentation, including workflow changes and failure repairs.
Assign writers matching the affected owners; do not create a Backend writer for frontend-only work.
The Senior resolves cross-owner decisions and assigns corrections to writers; it must not take over
edits or treat a child report as final acceptance. Scale model effort and checks to impact, and do
not add pointless tests for trivial text. Explanatory/read-only questions do not need writers.
Explicit newer user instructions and higher-priority host requirements still apply. If subagents
are unavailable, report the concrete blocker; never silently substitute inline roles or claim
that one identity provided independent acceptance. These are responsibilities, not eight mandatory
simultaneous processes; open only roles with useful work.

Before assigning work, identify independently useful outcomes, their owners, prerequisite
decisions and completion criteria. Delegate only ready tasks; a consumer may investigate in
parallel, but dependent implementation waits for the agreed contract or provider output.
Use the existing task/plan state to record assignments, dependencies and blocked/completed
status. The Senior can advance independent analysis and coordination while writers work.

Use optional specialists at the decision they inform, within the phase/concurrency budget below:
Explorer investigates before ownership assignment and task splitting when the root cause or owner
is unclear. Data/Migration assesses the timeline, preservation and recovery before schema,
migration, backfill, deletion or data-ownership decisions. Pipeline participates during planning
for build, deployment, proxy, secrets or CI work and returns for applicable final verification.
Inspect their outputs before dependent decisions and implementation proceed; these roles are not
an additional mandatory roster for every task.

| Role | Responsibility | Preferred model when available | Reasoning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Senior/Coordinator | Architecture, scope, shared contracts, assignment, integration, final evidence and authorized delivery | Current primary session model | High when configurable |
| Frontend | Owned typed UI, API consumers, loading, accessibility and focused coverage | `gpt-6.1-sol` | Medium by default; high for auth, contracts or complex state; xhigh for particularly complex interacting state, async or realtime races, or unresolved reasoning difficulty |
| Backend | Owned Rust/service/HTTP changes, authorization, persistence and focused coverage | `gpt-6.1-sol` | High by default; xhigh for complex transaction, concurrency, idempotency, migration preservation or cross-domain architecture reasoning |
| Rules/Security Reviewer | Independent read-only review of correctness, rules, permissions, secrets, tenant boundaries and the complete integrated diff | `gpt-6.1-sol` | High |
| QA/Verifier | Independently assess requirements and acceptance criteria, select coverage and run affected owned checks; report commands, candidate tree, environment and results without editing source | `gpt-6-luna`; `gpt-6.1-sol` for complex validation | Low for predefined check execution; medium for selecting/analyzing coverage; high on `gpt-6.1-sol` for complex authentication, authorization/tenant isolation, security, transactions or data migration validation |
| Explorer | Read-only implementation/call-site/dependency investigation with file and line evidence | `gpt-6-luna`; `gpt-6.1-sol` for architectural uncertainty | Low for file/call-site lookup; medium for multi-layer flow tracing; architectural investigation escalates to `gpt-6.1-sol`/medium |
| Data/Migration | Migration timeline, preservation, tenant isolation, reconciliation and recovery | `gpt-6.1-sol` | High |
| Pipeline | Build/cache/change selection, deployment, maintenance and acceptance flow | `gpt-6.1-sol` | High |

These are role defaults, not a claim that every host offers these models. For the Senior, recommend
`gpt-6.1-sol` with high reasoning when the user can select the primary session configuration.
A skill cannot change its own model; retain the current primary model and never spawn a second
Coordinator merely to simulate changing it. Explicit user model choices override these defaults.

For Frontend and Backend, xhigh is optional within supported host settings. The Senior may select
it upfront when evidence establishes the complexity above; a failed attempt at high is not required.
The Senior may also escalate during work with concrete uncertainty and bounded task context.
File count alone is not a criterion, and higher reasoning does not replace evidence or required checks.

Select profiles from the host's currently offered models and supported reasoning values. Create
subagents and pass the chosen model/reasoning through actual spawn controls, such as Codex's
`spawn_agent` when available. A new chat/thread is not a substitute for a subagent; role text and
`agents/openai.yaml` cannot create or configure agents. When overrides require fresh context,
start the child without inherited history and supply the bounded assignment explicitly. Record the requested
configuration, actual tool-reported settings and any fallback in task state; do not claim an
unconfirmed setting was applied or describe tool acceptance as independent provider/runtime
attestation. If a profile is unavailable, use a suitable offered alternative or inherit the primary
configuration and report the limitation once.

Bound escalation to the cause of uncertainty: investigate missing evidence at the authoritative
files, call sites or observed behavior; route environment/access failures to Pipeline when useful,
respecting the user's authorization and credential boundaries. Higher reasoning cannot supply
missing evidence or access. For reasoning/architecture difficulty, raise supported reasoning or
reassign to a more capable offered model when uncertainty, risk or stalled diagnosis warrants it.
Update the bounded assignment and revalidate affected evidence; do not repeat blind guesses.
QA selects and assesses coverage against requirements and acceptance criteria independently of
the writer's test list. QA reports failures to the Senior; diagnosis and fixes return to the
implementation owner, with separate Reviewer and QA acceptance preserved.

- Default parallel work to 2–4 active agents including the Coordinator, at most two implementation
  writers, and the host's lower concurrency limit. Use phases rather than opening the whole roster:
  Senior + one relevant writer (or Frontend + Backend for independent full-stack implementation),
  then Senior + Reviewer + QA/Verifier
  for the frozen candidate. Reuse a role's agent for follow-up within its configuration; changing
  the role label alone does not change the model. One implementation owner does not remove the
  independent final review and QA requirement above.
- Prefer the least costly available model capable of the assigned work. Configure model/reasoning
  only through controls supported by the current host; otherwise inherit its configuration and
  report that limitation. Roles do not guarantee model availability, token savings or speed.
- Before delegation, record the tested base, task, assigned branch/worktree, allowed paths,
  dependencies, protected shared files, focused checks and expected deliverable in chat/task state.
  Every child reads `.rules` and relevant canonical docs itself. Give only the needed context;
  do not assume inherited conversation includes the latest rules or user decisions.
- The Coordinator owns the delegation budget, spawning, dependency order and reassignment.
  Children report newly discovered work rather than starting unassigned agents. While a child
  works, the Coordinator advances independent work without duplicating that assignment.
  If an output is blocked, missing or incorrect, revise or reassign its bounded task before
  starting dependent work; do not silently treat partial completion as an agreed interface.
- If the host lacks subagent tools, report the concrete implementation blocker and available
  read-only progress; do not simulate separate writer, Reviewer or QA identities inline.
  Host instructions and actual supported controls take precedence over role preferences.
- In a shared checkout, assign non-overlapping file ownership before writers start. Frontend and
  Backend can work concurrently within their assigned files; stop the affected assignment and
  notify the Coordinator before crossing a boundary or touching another owner's changes. Never
  overwrite or revert another agent's work. Separate directories alone are not an ownership map.
- Shared files, generated contracts/locks, router composition, migration numbering and aggregate
  commands have one designated owner. The Coordinator serializes their changes and generators,
  dependency installation and broad formatters. Writers may use scoped formatters on owned files;
  no repository-wide rewrite or shared-output mutation runs while another writer depends on it.
- The Coordinator alone performs Git mutations in the shared checkout: staging, commits, branch
  switches, merges, stash/reset/clean, rebase and push. Children deliver owned changed paths,
  relevant checks and findings in place, without staging or committing other writers' files.
  If isolation is needed, record the assigned worktree/branch and integrate its commits only after
  the owner finishes; a separate worktree does not make conflicting edits safe to integrate.
- Explorer and Reviewer are read-only. Verifier may produce ignored test/build outputs and owned
  disposable fixtures, but does not fix source, install/update dependencies, run generators that
  change tracked files, or merge. Findings go to the Senior, who assigns diagnosis and fixes to
  the designated writer.
- Only the Coordinator reconciles shared changes or integrates isolated commits, manages shared Git state, opens/updates
  PRs, authorizes readiness or triggers production operations. Child completion is evidence for
  integration, not permission to push main, deploy or claim the full feature complete.
- Assign one Rust build/verification owner at a time. Reuse the existing compatible Cargo target
  sequentially; never run concurrent Cargo processes against it or create per-agent targets merely
  to evade serialization. Keep compiler job parallelism within the machine's available memory.
  Sharing a checkout avoids duplicate dependency/target disk usage but does not bound compiler RAM.
  Run focused checks only while their relevant source/configuration inputs are stable. Independent
  database test jobs use distinct disposable databases; reuse compiled outputs only with compatible
  toolchain, profile and dependency state.
- For every implementation change, including text and single-writer work, freeze the candidate
  tree and dispatch the separate read-only Rules/Security Reviewer and QA/Verifier in parallel
  when independent, with proportional checks. Source changes invalidate affected review/test results;
  freeze again and rerun affected checks. Review must cover the complete integrated diff.
- In the shared checkout, wait for every writer and source-mutating tool to finish before the
  Coordinator stages/freezes the complete candidate and dispatches final review and QA. Keep those
  inputs unchanged until both finish; a child cannot treat the live shared tree as its own commit.
- Handoffs report commit/tree IDs, changed paths, checks actually run, results, findings and remaining
  uncertainty. On compaction/resume, reread current rules and revalidate task/base/ownership/results.
  Keep incomplete checks or unresolved blocking findings in draft; never invent a passing result.
- Role prompts and allowed-path assignments are procedural controls, not a filesystem or credential
  security boundary. Use real host isolation where needed; do not claim a prompt restricts tools.

### Reports, failure recovery and completion

- Child reports are handoffs to the Senior, not user-facing task completion. Include the assigned
  outcome, changed paths or read-only findings, candidate/inputs, actual commands and results,
  problems encountered, their known causes, remaining work and uncertainty. Keep role and actual
  model/reasoning settings in task state. The Senior reconciles reports and assigns the next action;
  do not forward a child's final answer as the whole task's final answer.
- A failed required check or blocking review finding starts a repair loop: reports go to the
  Senior, who diagnoses the issue and assigns the correction to the responsible writer, inspects
  the proposed correction, freezes the new candidate,
  and obtains affected review and QA again. Continue until the required checks pass and blocking
  findings are resolved, or a concrete blocker genuinely prevents authorized progress. Escalate
  the model/reasoning or reassign the correction when diagnosis stalls; do not repeat guesses
  or let the Senior silently edit files itself. QA never fixes source; Reviewer acceptance and
  QA acceptance remain separate.
- A failure existing on main is not by itself a reason to stop. Reproduce and bound its impact;
  fix related or delivery-blocking defects when the correction is bounded, reversible and within
  the requested outcome, even if the failing file was not initially assigned. The Senior revises
  file ownership before edits and reruns affected checks. Do not expand into unrelated remediation,
  weaken tests, disable checks or narrow coverage to hide a required failure.
- A draft PR preserves progress while work continues; it is not a stopping condition. Report
  failures and pending work in progress updates while advancing independent authorized work.
  Pause dependent work only for a concrete blocker such as unavailable access/environment or a
  necessary decision changing product behavior, a public contract, data safety or approved scope.
  Explain the failed step, attempted diagnosis/fixes, impact and exact missing input or decision.
  A failed test, child report or pre-existing defect alone does not justify ending the turn.
- Ordinary implementation requests include PR delivery and eligible trusted-team integration
  under the existing gates unless the user restricts delivery. After local acceptance, mark the
  PR ready and track CI and automatic merge to a verified terminal result; do not ask again for
  an already-authorized merge or end with only "waiting for merge". If main advances, update and
  revalidate affected inputs; investigate failed CI/controller runs rather than bypassing gates.
  Follow any selected main Pipeline deployment and required acceptance to their terminal result
  within the existing delivery scope. Do not invent a deployment when the planner selects none.
  A real access, host or external-service blocker may require a truthful blocked handoff instead.
- The Senior ends with a concise user-facing summary: what changed and its user-visible result;
  which role did what and actual model/reasoning settings or fallbacks; problems encountered,
  known causes and resolutions; checks actually run and their results; PR/merge and applicable
  deployment/acceptance status; remaining blockers, limitations or work. State unresolved causes
  as unknown, and say when no problems were encountered. Keep this in chat and the PR, not a new
  tracked completion-report file. A blocked handoff must not claim the task is complete.

### Local verification and GitHub delivery

- Before opening or updating a ready PR, run the impact-based checks selected under section 11 on the developer's machine. Use focused owning commands or explicit pipeline scopes that match the affected behavior and consumers; the automatic application's entire suite is not mandatory for every edit. Record the scope rationale, exact commands, results and any missing environment in the PR; an unrun or cancelled check must not be reported as passing.
- GitHub runs planning, selected release builds and deployment only. Do not add unit, database, architecture, lint, type-check or browser E2E suites to Actions, and do not implement local receipt uploads, signatures, registered verifiers or test-result attestations. The `Pipeline gate` proves planning/build success, not local test execution.
- Each independent developer session owns its branch/worktree and disposable fixtures; a coordinated feature team may share its checkout under the ownership policy above. The Senior fetches `origin` and checks that `origin/main` is an ancestor of feature HEAD before final acceptance, readiness and integration, following the update/revalidation procedure above. When main advances, update the feature branch and rerun affected suites and review before making the PR ready; the merge controller must never update a branch or silently substitute untested source.
- Trusted same-repository team PRs may auto-merge with a merge commit after the latest-main `Pipeline gate` succeeds, with no mandatory human approval. Keep incomplete or failing work in draft; external/fork work needs maintainer-owned local verification before integration.
- Production readiness, tenant migrations, deployed browser/asset acceptance, authenticated proxy smoke and universal Maintenance Mode remain mandatory deployment gates. They verify the actual deployed artifacts and environment after selected builds; local tests do not replace them.

## 2. Adding or Changing a Feature

For every feature or behavior change:

1. Find the closest existing module and read its route, handler, service, model, frontend API wrapper, page loader, route metadata, and tests.
2. Define data ownership and authorization before implementing. Decide whether access is `own`, `assigned`, `organization_unit`, `organization_tree`, `school`, or another existing scope.
3. If the database changes, add a new sequential migration. Never edit an applied migration.
4. If authorization changes, update `contracts/permissions.json`, generate registries, enforce the permission and resource policy in the backend, add route/UI metadata, and test both allowed and denied paths.
5. If the JSON API changes, update typed Rust DTOs and OpenAPI registration, regenerate the contract, and consume generated TypeScript DTOs.
6. Put business logic in a service or pure helper with focused tests. Keep handlers limited to context, authorization, service calls, response formatting, and necessary realtime broadcasts.
7. Load only data needed for the current page or workflow. Patch affected client state after typed mutations instead of broadly reloading a workspace.
8. Update canonical documentation only when the durable system, verification, or operational procedure changed. Do not add a completion report.
9. Run all applicable checks from section 11.

Frontend flows, generated API contracts, backend rules and database constraints must express the same domain meaning for scope, context, required values, defaults, validation, lifecycle and persistence. When an approved domain change removes a context or field, remove silent backend inference, required lookup dependencies and stale database constraints at their authoritative owners; do not retain them merely because the UI no longer submits the value. Preserve existing facts and required history through an explicit reconciled migration.

A feature is not complete when only its UI is hidden. Backend authorization, resource scope, contract generation, migrations, tests, and operational impact are part of the same change.

Feature toggles are optional. Add one only for staged rollout or operational control, and enforce it in the backend as well as the UI; a frontend-only toggle is not an authorization or safety boundary.

### User-facing business codes

- Add a user-facing business code only when a demonstrable workflow or official reference requires it. Use names and relevant context by default, including for projects and activities; do not add a code merely because the record needs an identifier.
- Retain internal UUIDs, foreign keys, technical registries and existing required codes. This rule does not authorize incidental code removal, schema changes or migrations in unrelated domains.

### Canonical replacements and data preservation

- Design replacement schemas, APIs, DTOs, and business models around one orderly canonical structure suitable for long-term maintenance. Do not distort the new design merely to keep a legacy runtime interface alive.
- Persisted legacy data is migration input, not a runtime compatibility requirement. Preserve its valid meaning, stable identities, relationships, and required history through explicit transformation into the canonical model; do not silently discard values, guess ambiguous mappings, or create an indefinite duplicate archive.
- After cutover, the runtime has one canonical owner and path. Do not add new product behavior to a legacy path except when required to make migration safe, and remove legacy endpoints, fields, aliases, fallbacks, dual reads/writes, and duplicate schema owners after verified cleanup.
- Compatibility may exist temporarily only when a safe rollout or rollback requires it. The design must name its owner, exact scope, removal condition, and cleanup step before implementation.
- Data preservation does not override authorized retention, deletion, legal-hold, or PDPA requirements. Do not retain data longer than policy allows merely because it originated in a legacy model.

## 3. Backend: Rust, Axum, SQLx

Both backends keep HTTP handling, business decisions, and persistence in their owning layers. The crate, tenant-context, and feature-module paths below describe backend-school. Backend-admin uses its existing application/service owners; do not copy school tenant or permission adapters into control-plane handlers. Existing Admin deviations do not waive the general error, typing, security, or API requirements; repair a changed boundary with its actual callers and tests.

### Layer ownership and implementation references

For each endpoint, establish this flow before writing code: router/session middleware → typed handler → resource policy and domain service → database operation → typed HTTP envelope. Keep external delivery in the owning application adapter when the domain cannot depend on it.

| Responsibility | School owner and rule |
| --- | --- |
| Startup and router composition | [Application router](backend-school/src/app.rs) and application-owned state. Register the route under the existing authentication/tenant boundary; do not create a feature-local auth flow. |
| HTTP transport | The owning domain crate's `http/handlers.rs`, or its existing dedicated HTTP crate. Read [timetable handlers](backend-school/crates/school-academic-http/src/handlers/timetable_blocks.rs) for narrow HTTP state and typed service calls, or [calendar request handlers](backend-school/crates/school-calendar/src/http/request_handlers.rs) for a domain-owned transport. Neither is a license to copy an unrelated feature's state or permissions. |
| Business rules and persistence | The existing domain crate's typed models, policy, and service operations. Read [calendar requests](backend-school/crates/school-calendar/src/requests.rs) for validation, bounded reads, and transactional decisions. Keep related small capabilities as modules within their existing domain owner. |
| Resource scope | The owning domain policy or existing application policy. [Staff access policy](backend-school/crates/school-staff/src/staff_access_policy.rs) illustrates reuse of the shared resource policy and separate PII access; select the policy matching the actual resource. |
| Cross-domain side effects | Producers accept inbox notifications through `school-notifications::dispatch::create_in_transaction` inside the business transaction. [Calendar workflow](backend-school/crates/school-calendar/src/delivery/request_workflow.rs) only wakes presentation after commit; source-validity adapters live in application composition without a notification dependency back to Calendar. |
| Response and wire schema | [School HTTP types](backend-school/crates/school-http/src/lib.rs), typed domain DTOs, and [OpenAPI composition](backend-school/src/api_contract.rs). Follow section 5 for generation and consumers. |

Backend-admin equivalents are [router](backend-admin/src/app.rs), [handlers](backend-admin/src/handlers/), [services](backend-admin/src/services/), and [models](backend-admin/src/models/), consumed by [the Admin API client](frontend-admin/src/lib/api/client.ts). These links identify ownership, not blanket approval of existing implementation details. Inspect each changed endpoint's actual authentication, error mapping, wire types, and callers before reuse.

### Backend-school Cargo workspace

- `backend-school/Cargo.toml` is both the application package and the non-virtual workspace root.
  The application package owns startup, the router, full `AppState`, OpenAPI composition,
  schedulers, and cross-domain adapters; an internal crate must not depend on the root package or
  accept the full `AppState`. Domain HTTP crates own concrete handlers and their path declarations,
  receive narrow state through application-owned `FromRef` implementations, and use provider-owned
  ports for cross-domain operations. `school-auth-http` is the single session/tenant request-context
  and cookie/origin adapter owner; all callers use it directly without duplicating identity logic.
- Keep domain route factories non-generic over the application state and return
  `Router<DomainHttpState>`. Bind the existing narrow `FromRef` state at application composition,
  before the shared session middleware. This keeps handler futures, extractors and response
  encoding in the owning HTTP crate rather than regenerating them in the executable. Global
  prefixes, public/protected partitioning and cross-domain assembly remain application-owned.
- Keep feature HTTP handlers, route factories, DTOs and policies with their domain owner, normally
  under `crates/<owner>/src/http/`. Existing dedicated Academic, Certificate, Auth and File HTTP
  crates retain transport composition where importing it into a lower provider would create a
  dependency cycle. Do not retain production feature facades in the executable.
- Group related small capabilities as modules in an existing cohesive owner. Parents belongs to
  Students, Achievement to Staff, Work to Workflow, and menu/feature/route registration to Navigation.
  School administration owns its settings, facility, supply-catalog and lookup modules. Global
  prefixes and public/protected separation remain in `src/app.rs`; internal operational endpoints
  remain in `src/system`. Deliberate cross-domain adapters live in `src/adapters`.
- Subject catalog versions are append-only published revisions of one stable subject/code. Revisions have a civil start date and no expiry date; default display selects the highest revision at the latest eligible start date. Upcoming display selects the highest revision on the earliest future start date. Curriculum requirements and offerings retain their pinned version IDs. Reject exact semantic duplicates against the published revision applicable at the requested start date and other drafts under the stable-subject lock, including normalized decimal credit and unordered grade sets. Activity revisions use the same start-only display selection, existing stable-activity locks and revision-number uniqueness, with no interval-overlap or new content-deduplication policy.
- New curriculum course and activity tuples use only the catalog provider's current published revision of the same resource kind and stable identity as of Bangkok today: latest eligible effective start, highest revision, and no upcoming/draft fallback. Options retain all scoped published versions as metadata and expose stable catalog IDs plus new-row eligibility; structure rows carry their pinned stable ID and revision. Existing `(program, kind, version, grade, term)` tuples, including copies, retain their exact version when order or requirement kind changes. Lock affected stable subjects first, then activities, each in UUID order before new-row decisions, using the catalog owner's lock/current-selection APIs. Reject newly introduced duplicate stable subjects or activities within their resource kind in one program/grade/term; existing duplicate buckets may remain editable only when every submitted tuple in that bucket already existed. Explicit replacement leaving one row is allowed; never silently upgrade pinned version IDs.
- Curriculum structure reads, including historical publications, expose a read-time warning for subjects and activities only when the catalog provider selects a current published revision of the same resource kind and stable catalog identity with a greater revision number than the pinned version. Keep pinned metadata unchanged; upcoming, draft or archived revisions alone do not trigger the warning. Curriculum UI shows the warning as a yellow `เก่า` badge and omits revision-number labels.
- Application integration fixtures live in the test-only `src/integration_tests` namespace; shared
  historical migration fixtures belong to the development-only `school-test-db` crate. Unit tests
  remain with the owning implementation. Never compile integration fixtures into production.
- Evaluate crate placement whenever a capability is added or an existing capability materially
  expands. Create a crate only for a cohesive owner with a public interface materially narrower
  than its implementation, acyclic and intentionally directed dependencies, meaningful focused
  tests, and expected compile-scope value.
- When those admission criteria and compile-scope evidence show that a crate will materially
  improve ownership or invalidation scope, implement the crate boundary in the same change; crate
  evaluation is not optional. Otherwise keep the capability with its existing owner and record the
  reasoning in the approved design or review context.
- Keep small code with the same owner and lifecycle as an existing domain as a module in that
  owner. File count alone never justifies a crate.
- Prefer the smallest useful crate set. Do not create catch-all `common`, `shared`, or `utils`
  crates, and do not move unrelated code into a lower layer to conceal a dependency cycle.
- Internal crates receive only the state, typed data, or narrow port they require. Cross-domain
  behavior goes through a provider-owned public operation or an application-owned adapter.
- Select concrete cross-domain runtime and roster providers in application composition. Reuse the
  owning domain's narrow ports rather than importing an implementation merely to construct it.
  Students and Admission receive Core's `PlacementRosterPort`; preserve the caller's transaction,
  actor and affected IDs, and propagate provider errors before commit. Behavior refreshes Attendance
  through its application-bound runtime port only after successful commit, retaining best-effort
  refresh semantics. Behavior and Attendance must not depend on each other.
- Lookup wire DTOs belong with their lookup endpoint/query owner, even when they describe another
  domain's entities. Keep `OrganizationUnitLookupItem` in Administration's lookup module. Timetable
  receives Staff affiliations through its narrow directory port and the application adapter;
  keep Staff's authoritative query and Files' Staff authorization policies with Staff.
- Centralize shared dependency versions and deny-level lints in the workspace manifest. Every
  internal crate opts into workspace dependencies and lints; the application retains its existing
  crate-level deny lints and also opts into the workspace policy.
- Support a new crate boundary with before-and-after compile evidence on the same toolchain,
  machine, Cargo configuration, and dependency state. Record the ownership and invalidation-scope
  tradeoffs rather than assuming a split improves every build. It must not weaken runtime,
  API/OpenAPI, permission, migration, security, test, or
  deployment guarantees.
- Compile and test affected packages and their consumers. Use
  `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` when workspace composition, shared dependencies/build
  configuration, broadly consumed contracts or uncertain dependency impact require workspace coverage;
  a bounded domain edit does not automatically require every workspace package or database suite.

### Connection-bound PostgreSQL execution

- `school-database` owns the shared PostgreSQL query execution/observation boundary.
  Its `DbSession` receives only `&mut PgConnection`; query, typed-row and scalar
  builders retain that exclusive borrow and accept no replacement executor. Preserve
  SQLx 0.9 safe strings, binds, result types and `sqlx::Error`; native QueryBuilder
  results use the prepared adapters. Do not rebuild SQL or cast away safety checks.
- SQL, authorization, transactions, snapshots, advisory/row locks and domain error
  mapping remain with their existing owners. Acquire once at a declared work boundary,
  then pass the connection/session to helpers. No query helper may acquire another
  pool connection, accept a pool escape hatch, silently start a transaction or retry
  an ambiguous write. Explicit bootstrap/schema verification, SQLx migrator internals
  and disposable fixture setup retain narrow native-SQLx exceptions.
- Keep `school-database` below tenancy, migrations and all domains: production
  dependencies are SQLx and tracing only. `school-tenancy` owns observed checkout and
  logical lease retention; its connection guard lends only `PgConnection` and exposes
  explicit cancellation-safe return/close methods without detach/inner-pool access.
- Query observations use static source-owned operation labels and fixed duration,
  completion/cancellation status and safe error categories. Never record SQL, binds,
  raw database errors, URLs, credentials or personal data. Domain context logs may
  use the provider's `error_class`; preserve original errors for normal typed mapping.
- Resolve control-plane metadata before shared background database admission. Use
  tenancy's opaque validated work snapshots; validate the current cache value revision,
  expiry, fence and expected UUID before and after physical pool acquisition. Do not
  refetch HTTP under a database permit or silently retry a stale proof. Preserve the
  original whole-phase/item deadline across resolution, admission, pool wait and SQL;
  this local proof does not replace domain transaction/source/lease fences.
- Resource work entries choose observed checkout or owned `ObservedTransaction`
  explicitly. Connection/transaction helpers accept that existing connection and
  never acquire another pool connection. A pool-carrying repository factory may be
  constructed under a transaction only when construction performs no checkout and
  the called operation uses the borrowed transaction. Pure authorization/cache hits,
  empty inputs and validation failures must retain their original database-free path.
- Query duration measures the client await (driver/network/decoding included), not
  server execution time. Cancellation observations describe client future lifetime;
  they do not assert that PostgreSQL has already cancelled a statement. Transaction
  `begin_wait_us` includes acquisition and `BEGIN`; physical-budget admission,
  background admission, checkout and hold are distinct resource observations.

### Request context and architecture

- Share pools and configuration through the application state and cloneable narrow HTTP state.
  Domain HTTP handlers use concrete state rather than generic handler bodies, so their code compiles
  in the owning crate instead of being monomorphized back into the application.
- Authenticated browser handlers receive `AuthenticatedSession` from the single router-level session middleware. Feature handlers pass it to `actor_tenant_context_from_session` or `current_user_tenant_context_from_session`; they never parse `Cookie`, `Authorization`, session tokens, or CSRF headers.
- Public tenant handlers use `tenant_context`, `tenant_pool`, or `tenant_context_by_subdomain` as appropriate.
- Do not add handler-local pool, JWT, actor, or tenant-resolution helpers. Do not call `get_school_database_url`, `pool_manager.get_pool`, `PgPool::connect`, lower-level tenant resolvers, or `load_actor_context` directly from feature handlers. The explicit manual migration operation is the exception.
- Browser tenant resolution defaults to `Origin`/`Referer`. `X-School-Subdomain` is an explicit override for non-browser, local/custom-host, script, and test clients; if tenant origin data is present, both must match.
- Browser mutations require the exact tenant `Origin` plus `X-CSRF-Token`. The backend-host-only `__Host-schoolorbit_session` cookie is opaque, `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Lax`, `Path=/`, and has no `Domain` attribute.
- Keep handlers thin: request context → permission/policy → service → typed response → realtime signal when required.
- Feature handlers must not contain `sqlx::query*`, `.fetch_*`, `.execute`, `.begin`, `sqlx::FromRow`, or database helpers typed as `PgPool`.
- Business logic and SQL belong to the owning domain crate's service/model layer, including typed orchestration within that owner. DB-facing types stay with that owner, not in handlers. DB service operations receive `&PgPool` or the existing transaction interface and return `Result<T, AppError>` or a meaningful outcome enum; pure helpers need no pool.
- Extract validation, grouping, defaults, formatting, parsing, ID generation, and decision logic into testable helpers. New logic-bearing service files require focused `#[cfg(test)]` tests.
- Prefer validated bulk SQL for same-kind multi-row mutations. Normalize and deduplicate before writing. Per-row writes are reserved for genuinely different side effects or ordering.
- Never create `mod.rs`; use sibling module files such as `handlers.rs`.

### Errors, logging, and types

- Production request, background and startup paths must not use `unwrap()`, `expect()`, `panic!` or `unreachable!`. Represent valid states in types and propagate failures with useful context; startup must fail closed with an explicit error, a safe stage and actionable setting/error categories. Never format untrusted provider errors or environment values into startup diagnostics. Tests and disposable test fixtures may intentionally panic.
- The owning local verifier runs production-only Clippy guards for these four panic forms with `--no-deps`: School default and `api-docs` library/binary targets exclude only `school-test-db`; Admin library/binary targets run through its disposable database fixture. Keep tests usable and unrelated warning policy unchanged.
- School production bcrypt hash/verify work belongs to `school-crypto::password_work`, with one process-wide budget of four blocking jobs and at most one second of semaphore admission. Acquire before spawning and retain the permit inside the blocking closure through cancellation; zeroize owned plaintext. Return a safe 503 on saturation and preserve each caller's existing cost/truncation policy. Keep credential work outside database holds where authoritative identity/numbering permits, then revalidate mutable credential/account snapshots under the owning transaction's user lock before issuing a session. Admin keeps its own application-owned worker; do not couple the applications through a shared runtime queue.
- Do not discard meaningful errors with `let _ =`. Map them into `AppError`/`IntoResponse` and preserve actionable client messages without leaking internals.
- Runtime logging uses `tracing::{debug, info, warn, error}`. `println!`/`eprintln!` are only for intentional CLI output.
- Never log plaintext PII, national IDs, credentials, tokens, cookies, database URLs, encryption keys, or raw request bodies.
- API requests and responses use typed `serde` structs, normally with `#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]`.
- Stable-shape JSON/JSONB uses named domain types or `sqlx::types::Json<T>`. `serde_json::Value` is limited to genuinely dynamic metadata, flexible configuration, audit records, or third-party payloads.
- A service feeding a JSON endpoint returns typed structs/outcomes, not ad-hoc `serde_json::Value`.
- Prefer compile-time-checked SQLx queries where practical.

### Validation, transactions, and side effects

- Validate client input in the backend even when the form already validates it. Define required/optional/null fields, lengths, allowed values, date ranges, and state transitions in typed domain validation. Derive actor identity and protected ownership from authenticated context; never accept a client-selected actor or tenant as authorization.
- Parameterize SQL values. Dynamic sort directions, identifiers, or clauses come from a closed server-owned mapping, never direct interpolation of request text. Bound page sizes and date ranges, use deterministic ordering with a stable tie-breaker, and preserve resource-scope predicates before pagination.
- When several writes form one business decision, use one transaction, including required durable audit records. Enforce uniqueness and relationships with database constraints as appropriate. Use existing row locks or revision checks for concurrent decisions; stale or already-decided resources must not silently overwrite current state or create duplicate outcomes.
- Define replay behavior for non-idempotent mutations. Do not automatically retry creation, approval, publication, or deletion after an ambiguous transport failure unless the operation has a verified idempotency design; reconcile authoritative state instead.
- Accept authoritative inbox rows atomically with business mutations through the central typed notification API. Emit realtime signals and attempt best-effort presentation only after commit and irreversible pending consumption. Presentation failure never reverses or misreports committed business acceptance. Record aggregate actionable diagnostics without private payloads, endpoints or keys.
- Keep civil dates and local times distinct from timestamps using the owning domain's typed date/time model. Reuse existing timezone and academic-context rules; do not silently convert a school calendar date to a UTC instant or infer year/term ownership from an unrelated UI selection.
- Cover validation, denied access, rollback, stale/concurrent writes, and retry behavior when relevant to the changed operation. Database and external-provider fixtures remain disposable and owned; do not exercise mutations or notifications against real tenant users.

### SQL read efficiency

- Backend read paths must minimize database round trips while preserving authorization, consistency, bounded results, and maintainable domain ownership. A lower request or query count is not sufficient evidence when one larger operation costs more.
- Select only fields used by the typed result. Normalize and deduplicate identifiers, eliminate N+1 reads, and batch same-shape lookups with bounded set-based SQL.
- Prefer joins, filtered aggregates, and pre-aggregated CTEs over repeated correlated work when representative query plans support the change.
- Start only genuinely independent reads concurrently and keep their concurrency within the tenant pool capacity. Keep reads sequential when one requires the exact result of another.
- Do not repeat the same context, authorization, or identifier-resolution read inside one request when it can be safely reused or combined without creating an oversized cross-domain query.
- Substantiate performance claims with endpoint phase timing and representative `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` evidence from a safe environment. Add an index only when predicates, ordering, cardinality, and the query plan justify it, and always through a new migration.

### API Response Contract

Application JSON endpoints use one envelope:

```text
success: true, data: T, message?: string
success: false, error: string, message?: string, data?: T
```

Private `/internal/` control-plane endpoints retain named, typed operational wire DTOs consumed by the deployment and tenancy owners. Their authenticated protocol is separate from public application responses; new credential endpoints must remain private and purpose-specific.

Use `ApiResponse` and `ApiErrorResponse` for application responses; do not return raw feature DTOs or ad-hoc top-level JSON. Non-JSON downloads, binary/image responses, WebSocket upgrades, and low-level health probes are exceptions. Empty mutations use `ApiResponse::empty()` or `ApiResponse::empty_with_message(...)`; success text belongs in `message`, not `data`.

## 4. Permissions and Resource Authorization

### Permission contract

- `contracts/permissions.json` is the permission source of truth.
- Codes use `module.action.scope`, and each code must match its `module`, `action`, and `scope` fields. Generated constant names mirror the code in uppercase.
- Prefer existing scopes: `own`, `assigned`, `organization_unit`, `organization_tree`, `school`, and `global`. Do not create shortened or role-named permission codes.
- Organization permissions use `organization_unit` and `organization_work`; do not add `department`, `dept`, `DEPT_*`, or `.department` variants.
- To add, rename, or remove a permission:
  1. Edit `contracts/permissions.json`.
  2. Add a new tenant migration to insert/rename/deactivate DB permission data and grants when applicable.
  3. From `frontend-school`, run `npm run generate:permissions`.
  4. Commit the contract lock and both generated registries.
  5. Use backend constants from `backend-school/crates/school-permissions/src/registry.rs` and frontend constants from `frontend-school/src/lib/permissions/registry.ts`.
  6. Add backend enforcement, resource-policy tests, route metadata, and capability-level UI checks.
  7. Run `npm run check:permissions` and `npm run test:permissions`.
- Generated files are artifacts; do not edit generated files directly.
- Raw permission strings are allowed only in seed/migration data, generator tests, invalid-input parsing tests, or explicit contract tests.

### Resource authorization

- Frontend route guards and hidden controls are convenience UX; backend policies are authoritative.
- Use `ActorContext` helpers for coarse capability checks. Do not reintroduce legacy permission wrappers or manual wildcard parsing.
- Access to another user or resource must be decided by a reusable policy in the owning domain crate. Shared scope primitives belong to `school-authorization`.
- Use `school_authorization::resource_access_policy` for shared organization membership/tree decisions instead of duplicating SQL.
- List endpoints combine independent scopes as a union. `own`, `assigned`, `organization_unit`, and `organization_tree` must not hide one another. Only a true `school`/all-record scope may short-circuit.
- Add focused policy/service tests for allowed, denied, and multi-scope union behavior.
- Organization grants apply to the exact unit and matching position. Do not infer parent/child permission inheritance; grant and enforce `*.organization_tree` explicitly.
- Central subject and activity catalogs are independent of academic years. Their stable identities and start-only revisions do not require or infer an academic year from an effective date and never expire. Keep subject semantic duplicate checks, stable catalog locks, revision uniqueness and immutable published/pinned versions. Current display selects the latest published start date on or before the selected civil date, then highest revision; upcoming display selects the earliest future start date, then highest revision. Year and term context belongs only to consumers whose own domain requires it, such as actual offerings and academic lifecycle; curriculum editions retain their independent semantics. Explicit published offering pins remain valid when their start date is no later than the selected term start. Former year bindings and expiry dates are provenance only, with no runtime relationship or validation authority.
- `subject_groups` owns learning-area names, status and order; its transactional database projection owns the linked organization row. Ordinary learning areas attach directly to `SCHOOL`. Create them only through the registry, preserve AC/ACAD-ACT, and never infer administrative-tree access across that separation.
- Organization/learning-area codes are server-generated and immutable; preserve existing codes and use UUIDv7 for new business identities.
- `SCHOOL` remains the root organization unit. Display membership derives recursively from direct administrative assignments without persisting ancestor copies or inheriting exact-unit permissions. `is_primary` is the HR/home assignment, not an authorization source; one primary learning-area assignment (`unit_type = subject_group`) and one primary administration assignment (all other unit types, including linked activity divisions) may coexist. Primary assignments in the same domain must not overlap in their effective dates.
- PII access is separate from ordinary profile access. Generic lookup endpoints must never expose national IDs, blind indexes, unnecessary contact data, medical/guardian-sensitive fields, or document identifiers.
- After roles, grants, delegations, memberships, or permission definitions change, invalidate the relevant backend permission cache and emit `permission_changed`. The frontend then silently refreshes `/api/auth/me`; SSE/Web Push payloads are signals, not permission truth.

### Staff identity and qualifications

- Store Thai and English title, given name and family name as independent raw values. Creating staff and changing their name require one complete language set; never combine languages or guess missing values. Unrelated edits may retain an incomplete historical name. Display a complete Thai set first, otherwise a complete English set; preserve an incomplete historical name for review until corrected. Thai prefixes attach to the given name; English parts are separated by spaces.
- Use the database's `resolve_person_name`/`user_display_names` read projection and the returned display name for staff identity consumers. Write raw fields through their owning service. Search both languages and order by the chosen given/family name with a stable ID before pagination. The shared [name fields](frontend-school/src/lib/components/staff/StaffNameFields.svelte) show both languages and the preferred display preview; examples: complete `นาย / สมชาย / ใจดี` takes precedence over `Mr. / Somchai / Jaidee`, while `Ms. / Alice / Example` works without Thai fields.
- Staff title controls offer Thai and English selections plus an explicit custom entry. Preserve historical/custom titles without silently replacing them; an unspecified selection remains empty and follows the complete-language validation above.
- Teaching-licence attachments are staff-owned private documents delivered through Files with both profile and PII authorization. Stage uploads against an existing staff resource, retain them only on a validated save, guard replacement with the expected current file, and prevent generic deletion of attached files. Preview PDF pages in a responsive spread with navigation and images as the uploaded image; load the renderer only when opened.
- Qualifications belong to the staff-owned collection, with stable row IDs and a collection revision. Omitted updates retain the collection; an explicit empty collection clears it. Validate ownership and stale revisions in the parent transaction. New rows require a level; preserved partial historical rows remain visible for correction. Render all qualifications from lower to higher recognized tiers, keeping equal tiers equal and other/unset levels separate at the end. Aggregate highest-level summaries derive from the highest recognized tier rather than another editable scalar; classify other-only qualifications separately from missing levels. For example, show bachelor → master → doctorate → other even when entered in another order. Use [education fields](frontend-school/src/lib/components/staff/StaffEducationFields.svelte) for create/edit, and confirm removal of saved rows before changing the draft.

## 5. API Contracts

The generated OpenAPI/TypeScript workflow below owns the School API. Admin changes must trace their typed Rust request/response models to the actual frontend-admin API consumer and test that boundary; do not claim a generated Admin contract exists or reuse School wire DTOs for unrelated control-plane data. Adding an Admin generator requires an explicit architectural change.

- Rust DTOs plus domain-owned `utoipa::path` declarations own the HTTP wire contract.
  `backend-school/src/api_contract.rs` owns composition and registration of the complete document.
- School documentation metadata is opt-in through the default-off `api-docs` Cargo feature.
  Each schema/path owner gates its Utoipa dependency, imports, derives and helper attributes;
  forward the feature along existing dependency edges. Keep runtime DTOs, Serde and HTTP behavior
  available without it. Application composition and offline export require this feature, and the
  local generator enables it automatically. Verify both feature modes, affected documentation-specific
  tests and generated artifacts; production builds keep the default feature set.
- Tracked OpenAPI output lives at `contracts/openapi/school-api.json`; generated frontend DTOs live under `frontend-school/src/lib/api/generated/`.
- When adding or changing an endpoint:
  1. Add typed request/response DTOs and `ToSchema` as needed.
  2. Annotate the handler with `utoipa::path`.
  3. Register paths and schemas in `api_contract.rs`.
  4. From `frontend-school`, run `npm run generate:api-contracts`.
  5. Consume the generated wire DTO in the frontend API module.
  6. Run `npm run check:api-contracts` and `npm run test:api-contracts`.
- Generated files are artifacts; do not edit generated files directly.
- Frontend API wrappers must use concrete response types. Do not use `unknown`, `ApiResponse<unknown>`, response casts, or `Record<string, unknown>` for a known shape. Give genuinely dynamic JSON a named contract.
- Use a separate UI view model and explicit mapper only when UI semantics differ from the wire DTO.

## 6. Database Migrations

- Migration files are immutable after application, including comments. SQLx checks checksums.
- Add a new next-numbered migration for every schema, index, data, permission, seed, or comment change.
- If a checksum mismatch follows an edit, restore the original file. Never patch `_sqlx_migrations` to conceal it.
- Runtime tenant migrations start at `backend-school/migrations/001_baseline.sql`. `backend-school/migrations_legacy/` is audit history only.
- Once any live tenant has applied the baseline, do not edit it; continue with `002_*.sql`, `003_*.sql`, and so on.
- Never point a clean-baseline release at a tenant whose `_sqlx_migrations` still contains the legacy timeline.
- New tenant provisioning and release deployments use `school_migrations::run_tenant_migrations` from `backend-school/crates/school-migrations/src/lib.rs`; do not introduce a second tenant migration path.
- The legacy rebaseline workflow is retired. If a tenant with legacy `_sqlx_migrations` history is discovered, stop and create a new reviewed recovery plan instead of reusing one-time tooling or improvising a cutover.
- For destructive or semantic replacements, use `expand → migrate/backfill → reconcile → switch consumers → contract/cleanup`, scaled to the affected data and runtime risk. Use new sequential forward migrations for every database-changing phase; implement reconciliation and consumer switching as explicit executable gates in the migration and deployment flow.
- Make transformations deterministic and safely retryable under the migration runner and recovery design. Before destructive cleanup, verify current aggregate counts, stable identities, relationships, appropriate checksums, and business invariants.
- If any source value cannot be mapped unambiguously or reconciliation is absent, stale, incomplete, or failed, it blocks both consumer cutover and destructive cleanup. Report only bounded finding codes and aggregate counts; never discard or guess data or expose raw rows, PII, credentials, secrets, or database URLs. Correct the data through an explicit reviewed remediation or recovery plan, then reconcile again.
- Database-backed tests use `TEST_DATABASE_URL` and isolated test state. Routine backend-school
  database tests run through `scripts/test_backend_school.sh`, which supplies PostgreSQL in a
  uniquely named Docker container and removes that exact container and its anonymous
  volumes after the command, including handled failure and signal paths. PostgreSQL migration/schema compatibility uses the same owned Docker server and fresh
  databases; no provider branch or production connection is a test fixture.

### PostgreSQL VPS ownership and resource policy

- PostgreSQL 18 is the database baseline; local fixtures pin the approved 18.6 image digest.
- Database-per-school provisioning belongs to Admin. Persist a stable tenant identity and
  authenticated encrypted credentials before creating resources; check role markers and
  database ownership before retry, adoption, compensation or deletion. An ambiguous create
  retains the failed control-plane record for reconciliation.
- Runtime roles are DML-only and never run migrations, permission reconciliation or DDL.
  Admin migrations run as explicit owner jobs; tenant provisioning/deployment uses the
  canonical tenant runner and grants the matching runtime role after successful migration.
  Verify immutable SQLx history before accepting a new runtime pool. Bound pool
  initialization (including tenant gate/schema reads) and graceful drainage; never
  hold the tenant creation gate while waiting for borrowers to drain. Reject closed
  cached handles, fail deployment closed on timeout, and never replay ambiguous writes.
  Verify initial schema on an owned connection before admitting it to the pool, so
  cancelling initialization closes its socket without an orphaned pool-return task.
- Public Admin DTOs exclude credentials and provider IDs. Tenant passwords are random and
  stored with AES-256-GCM under the separate `DATABASE_CREDENTIALS_KEY`, authenticated to
  the tenant UUID, purpose and envelope version. Internal migration credentials are fetched
  only by the privileged migration path; never cache owner pools in runtime managers.
- Production connections require verify-full TLS and the approved CA. Only the backend
  host reaches TCP 5432; human database management uses SSH and separate operator roles.
- New persisted business IDs use UUIDv7; preserve existing identities, deterministic UUIDv5,
  externally provided IDs, security tokens and fixtures. Default changes use forward migrations.
- Runtime tenant pools use `school-tenancy::TenantPool` logical leases and the shared
  aggregate physical reservation budget. Keep the lease alive across every database
  operation, including gaps between queries; raw pool clones passed to detached work
  must retain the originating lease before spawning. Dormant realtime/scheduler state
  reacquires before database work. Release pool reservations only after physical
  drainage, including cancellation and failed initialization. Owner/provisioning
  connections use their separate bounded reserve, never the runtime cache.
- Tenant directory caches retain only runtime credentials, with bounded age/capacity,
  single-flight fills and stale revision rejection. Share each fill's successful or
  failed outcome with its waiting cohort without persistent negative caching;
  cancellation wakes waiters and releases bounded distinct-key admission. Recheck
  revision/fences when followers consume completed results. Application status/credential
  mutations persist a durable Admin barrier before fencing School; claim the exact
  token and change school state in one transaction. Fences never expire open and
  stale completion cannot release a newer operation. Internal all-tenant lists fail
  closed while a relevant barrier is pending.
- Bound connection pools, cache entries/revision maps, background batches and log retention.
  Calendar, Auth retention, Notification dispatch, Attendance maintenance and File
  Platform reconciliation share bounded database-phase admission owned by `PoolManager`
  in `school-tenancy`. Consumers use that manager's executor, bound pending futures and
  exclude the same tenant/job kind without occupying a shared slot for exclusion.
  A same-key turn may span external I/O, but database permits and logical pool leases
  cover only its database phases: owned DTOs cross the network boundary, then tenant
  UUID/directory fences and exact device versions are checked again before completion effects. Each
  concurrent work item's database phase consumes its own shared permit. Domains
  retain business transactions, durable work, schedules and progress. Calendar and
  File reconciliation cron sweeps exclude overlaps. Calendar keeps its advisory-lock
  session through event/recipient reads and one atomic bulk acceptance/sent-at transaction on that same
  connection; never expose partial recipient acceptance. Confirm unlock before session reuse.
  Calendar advances a stable keyset past failed/contended reminders. Auth advances its
  tenant rotation before admission so cancellation cannot repeatedly starve the suffix.
  Notification uses 32-item batches and four item futures, with no whole-item deadline that can
  starve later eligible devices. Each Push endpoint has a two-second deadline and four-endpoint
  concurrency; admission caps active devices at twenty per user and releases DB permits before I/O.
  Permanent source/inbox identity conflicts terminally block the exact pending slot without transport; transient pre-consumption DB failures defer the tenant probe fifteen seconds. SSE runs independently before subscription lookup; Push failure cannot suppress or replay SSE.
  The notification device/session eligibility adapter mirrors Auth validity (revocation, idle/absolute expiry and active account) without reselecting or cancelling accepted inbox recipients. Session-bound devices expire ninety days after browser confirmation; identical refresh preserves
  version/binding token and actual identity/key/session changes rotate both. Automatic refresh supplies an exact expected binding version and cannot recreate a revoked/missing binding; only an explicit user enable may register anew. Public runtime key
  configuration owns VAPID identity. Validate HTTPS provider endpoints and crypto encodings before
  storage and revalidate endpoints before network I/O. Only 404/410 remove the exact attempted
  device/version; 401/403/global config, 400/413, 429/5xx/network/timeouts preserve registrations and
  end the current message. Generic lockscreen payloads carry device/version/binding and expiry
  for local account fencing; never log keys, endpoint URLs or private notification content.
  Cleanup removes at most 100 expired/revoked bindings per tenant turn; terminal pending refs
  disappear when consumed, while canonical receipts and unread inboxes remain durable.
  File worker metadata effects require the exact unexpired owner/attempt claim;
  external work cannot retain a database permit or write through a stale lease.
  File claim/finish DB phases are bounded within the existing lease, and the per-tenant
  soft budget starts at first admission and is checked after completed operations.
  A narrowly owned exception permits queuing a new durable delete after a successful
  or uncertain derivative PUT only for the same cancelled attempt whose file is being
  deleted; never revive its metadata or change a newer/expired/reclaimed claim.
  External storage writes remain nontransactional with PostgreSQL. Persist an independent,
  immutable physical-locator watch and verified provider namespace before every permitted PUT,
  including synchronous originals/derivatives and leased worker writes. Watch identities survive
  business hard purge without file/domain foreign keys; revocation is irreversible. Only revoked
  verified locators may be cleaned, with exact owner/generation/expiry completion fences and no DB
  resources during provider I/O. With no provider settlement proof, reschedule after DELETE or HEAD
  absence indefinitely; a later success never erases an older uncertain write. Mixed ordinary/watch
  work shares the existing batch/budget and persists starting opportunities and claim-time due progress.
  Legacy NULL namespaces fail closed; bind only with independently attested historical routing,
  immutable locator digest, expected mutable revision, all-row validation and actual table-owner
  authority. A writable custom GUC alone is never authorization. The read-only rollout gate checks
  every target in an independently trusted complete tenant inventory, including database identity,
  latest schema and all retained watches; it never migrates or silently adopts current routing.
  File attachment validates under the caller transaction's file lock before promotion. Expiry deletion
  revalidates temporary/expiry/lifecycle after that same lock, so a stale candidate cannot revoke a
  promoted accepted file. Never age-clean standard, referenced or historical files.
  Keep synchronous upload/delete semantics with the File Platform provider.
  Revision pruning must reject stale async fills. Session/file retention uses existing domain
  owners; never delete business history merely to free disk. Autovacuum reuses dead row space
  and is not business retention, RAM cleanup or a guarantee that files shrink.
- Monitor aggregate query/I/O statistics, route-template timings and pool wait, never credentials,
  SQL parameters or PII. Disable utility/parameter statement logging and driver SQL text.
- The completed Neon transfer has no executable cutover CLI, partial Admin migrator,
  release operation or runtime fallback. Recover on PostgreSQL through pgBackRest;
  a future provider move requires a new reviewed plan. Preserve applied migrations.
- Backup uses encrypted off-host pgBackRest chains with archived WAL. A successful restore
  of actual migrated data is a cutover gate. Keep source/config/key recovery material private.
- Provider cutover preserves `_sqlx_migrations` checksums and all data under maintenance.
  Fresh executable reconciliation gates credential retirement and consumer switching. No
  automatic source fallback is allowed after target writes; recover/roll forward on PostgreSQL.

## 7. Frontend: SvelteKit 3 / Svelte 5

Both frontends use their pinned SvelteKit/Svelte versions and owning configuration. School permission/menu metadata, generated DTOs, and the shared layout/UI paths below belong to frontend-school. In frontend-admin, reuse the local API/state/component owners and [theme](frontend-admin/src/routes/layout.css); do not import School components or fabricate School route metadata to achieve visual consistency.

### Data, state, and components

- Configure SvelteKit through `sveltekit({...})` in `vite.config.ts`. Use package subpath imports (`#lib/*`) with explicit module extensions, `$app/env`, and `$app/state`; do not restore retired Kit 2 configuration or aliases.
- UI primitives may use Runed's portable utilities. Do not import `runed/kit` while its Kit integration still depends on retired Kit 2 APIs; the scoped peer override does not make those utilities compatible.

- Use TypeScript throughout and keep the API boundary strictly typed.
- New or coherently rewritten Svelte components use typed `$props`, `$state`, and `$derived` for local reactive state and event properties such as `onclick`. Do not mix legacy `$:`/`export let`/`on:click` patterns into the same component or convert unrelated components as a side effect.
- Use derived state for computed values. Reserve `$effect` for synchronization with external state or browser resources, with disposal for listeners, subscriptions, timers, and pending work; do not use effect chains to duplicate authoritative state or trigger primary route reads.
- Prefer `+page.server.ts` loaders/actions where server-only credentials or server-side behavior are involved. Load route-specific data only.
- Reuse the owning shared client-state API for auth, permissions, realtime connectivity, notifications, and toasts. School uses its existing Svelte stores; Admin auth uses [its rune-based owner](frontend-admin/src/lib/stores/auth.svelte.ts). Do not add a second store or convert the owner just to match local component state.
- Type component props. Prefer existing local components and patterns before adding abstractions.
- Use Tailwind CSS with the owning theme and the standard controls specified in `Required School UI primitives`. Compose pagination and other missing higher-level controls from actual local exports rather than assuming an upstream component exists here.
- Large browser-only PDF, spreadsheet, image-conversion, and document-export libraries must load lazily after a user action and resolve to a lightweight server stub during SSR. Do not bundle them into the Cloudflare Worker.
- Dense fixed-format inputs need explicit readable column and input widths. Prefer horizontal scrolling to unreadable shrinking.
- Use shared app states from `src/lib/components/app-state/`: `PageSkeleton`, `PageState`, and `LoadingButton`.
- Mutation controls use action-specific loading state. When a typed mutation returns a resource, patch only the affected local state; reserve broad reloads for intentionally broad dependencies or manual refresh.

### Frontend file ownership

| Responsibility | Owning file or component |
| --- | --- |
| Route identity, context, and primary reads | `+page.ts` / the narrowest `+layout.ts`, typed load output, and School `_meta`. For a focused example read [school-font loader](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/school-fonts/+page.ts) and [calendar loader](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/calendar/+page.ts); reuse their responsibility boundaries, not their domain-specific permissions. |
| Route presentation and composition | `+page.svelte` composes the header, independently renderable regions, and workflow actions. Use generated route `PageProps` when consuming load data; do not duplicate transport or domain rules inside the markup. |
| Reusable domain UI | `src/lib/components/<domain>/` owns typed props, domain presentation, and interaction-specific state. Extract cohesive forms, dialogs, grids, and editors when they have an independent responsibility; file length alone is not a reason to create a generic abstraction. |
| API transport and wire types | `src/lib/api/<feature>.ts` uses the owning central client; School imports generated wire types. Pages/components call typed feature operations, including lazy interaction reads, instead of reimplementing cookies, CSRF, tenant headers, or HTTP envelope parsing. |
| Pure calculations and draft transformations | The existing feature helper owner under `src/lib/`. Keep these typed and independently testable; do not bury sorting, date normalization, payload mapping, or validation in repeated markup expressions. |
| Shared identity and app state | Existing auth/permission/realtime stores and app-state components. Feature drafts and per-action pending state stay local unless a shared owner is required. |

- For School streamed reads, reuse [route result handling](frontend-school/src/lib/navigation/route-load.ts). For superseded browser reads, reuse [LatestRequest](frontend-school/src/lib/async/latest-request.ts) when its lifecycle matches the feature; cancel/dispose and reject late results after route-context, user, or permission changes. Do not reset a user's active draft merely because unrelated load data refreshed.
- Use frontend validation for useful field feedback while retaining backend authority. Reuse the feature's existing schema/normalizer; if none exists, choose typed helpers or the installed Zod according to complexity. Align required/null, length, date, and enum semantics with the wire contract rather than inventing frontend-only business restrictions.

### Route data loading and navigation performance

- Load primary route data from `+page.ts` or the narrowest applicable `+layout.ts`; do not defer primary reads to component `onMount`. The route loader remains the owner of primary route data. `onMount` remains valid for browser-only listeners, observers, and interaction-only features.
- Use the SvelteKit load event's `fetch` through typed feature API modules so safe read navigation can benefit from data preloading and dependency tracking. Preloaded operations must be bounded, idempotent GET requests with no state-changing side effects.
- Keep required academic context in menu destinations so hover preloading can issue the primary read. A navigation control that is not an ordinary link must preload and navigate to the same resolved destination.
- Resolve required academic year and term before normal menu preload and navigation from the current valid selection or already-loaded active context. URL repair is a fallback for direct, stale, or external links; while fallback resolution is pending, render a neutral route skeleton instead of a false prerequisite.
- Keep hover preload for safe, reasonably sized, stable reads. Use tap or disable data preload for expensive or highly volatile routes where false-positive traffic or staleness is material.
- Divide primary route data by independently renderable UI region. Each region must be able to render, fail, retry, refresh, and reconcile mutations independently.
- Start all visible independent region reads concurrently from the SvelteKit route loader and expose typed in-flight results so one slow region does not block successful siblings. Sequential reads require a real data dependency; visual order is not a dependency.
- Public discovery lists only active, non-reserved school names/subdomains with bounded keyset pagination. Select no credentials, account, subscription, database or provider metadata, and never open tenant databases for discovery. Generate HTTPS tenant links from configured domains; keep School homepages tenant-canonical and sandbox/private/account pages non-indexable. The central sitemap contains only its own public pages and stays independent of directory/backend reads; crawlable directory links discover each school's existing sitemap. Preserve truthful 503/Retry-After during maintenance or genuine service failure instead of publishing error content as an indexable 200.
- Search branding uses the Admin Orbit mark as the platform fallback; its vector source is owned by `frontend-admin/static/schoolorbit.svg`, with matching square PNG assets in both independently deployed frontends. School homepage titles use `<school name> - ระบบจัดการโรงเรียนครบวงจร`; their description prefixes the actual public name with โรงเรียน only when it is absent and reads `โรงเรียน<name> เชื่อมครู นักเรียน และผู้ปกครอง ผ่านระบบจัดการโรงเรียนครบวงจร`. Preserve the actual name and use a truthful generic fallback when identity is unavailable. Each School host's stable `/favicon.png` resolves only its current public crest, selected by the School public favicon endpoint in one backend request and converted by the File Platform into a square PNG without cropping or changing the source. Apply existing tenant, public visibility, ready lifecycle, purpose and inspected-image limits before reading or decoding bytes; never expose private, quarantined or arbitrary caller-selected files. Missing branding falls back to the matching-size platform PNG; service failures remain uncached retryable failures. Installed School apps, Apple touch icons and notification icons use the same current-crest resolver at 192/512 pixels through `/app-icon/<size>.png`; manifest dimensions must match actual PNGs. Only declare maskable purpose when a separately verified safe-zone image exists. Keep the push-only worker network-only and preserve its registration scope/update behavior.
- Social sharing uses a separate 1200×630 PNG at the current School origin's `/og-image.png`, backed by the anonymous `/api/school/public/share-image` domain operation. School Administration selects only the current public school name and configured crest; File Platform validates/reads that crest and owns banner rendering with the Admin platform mark as the missing-crest fallback. Never accept caller-selected files or URLs. Keep this card separate from the square favicon, PWA icons and `School.logo`. Advertise wide image metadata and `summary_large_image` for the owned card, preserve explicit route image overrides, version homepage image URLs from public branding/template state, omit session cookies, bound timeout/PNG size, reject redirects, cache successful images for five minutes and keep genuine failures uncached and retryable. Image generation must not block homepage HTML.
- The public school homepage awaits only its bounded school identity read for server-rendered H1 and SEO metadata (at most eight seconds). Start the statistics read concurrently and stream it with independent errors and retries; it must not become a prerequisite for the initial HTML. Public personnel pages own their organization read independently of homepage statistics.
- Keep inactive tabs, dialogs, exports, expanded details, and action-only data lazy.
- Keep a directory's selected view, applied filters, sort and page/cursor in its URL so reload, browser history and return from details restore the same context. Switching only between list/table and card views reuses the loaded result and preserves filters and pagination without fetching the primary list or individual profiles again. In School's installed Kit 3, use `goto(url, { shallow: true })` for this presentation-only change and read the effective URL from `page.shallow?.url ?? page.url`; changes to data filters or paging still use the owning loader. Read the [staff directory](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/manage/+page.svelte) and [return navigation helper](frontend-school/src/lib/navigation/staff-management.ts) for this boundary.
- Give each region a focused skeleton, safe error, retry, cancellation or supersession behavior, and mutation reconciliation rule. Name an invalidation dependency when invalidation is selected, and do not use it when rerunning the owning loader would refetch unrelated regions.
- A region with no usable data and a pending read must enter its loading state before the first DOM paint and render its focused skeleton. Clear data from a different route context before that context is painted; never expose a transient blank or stale region while waiting for a post-render effect.
- When hover or tap preload has already resolved a region before its first paint, render the data immediately; do not force a minimum skeleton duration or add artificial delay. In `frontend-school`, during a background refresh with usable data, keep that data visible, retain `aria-busy`, and update silently without loading labels, spinners, badges, toasts, new layout rows, or replacement skeletons. Do not add routine page or region refresh controls; browser reload remains available. Preserve focused error-only retries, initial loading states, mutation progress, and explicit domain operations such as source synchronization or readiness checks.
- Do not create route-wide `/page-view` or equivalent generic batch endpoints. A cohesive typed domain endpoint remains valid when one independently renderable UI region needs all returned fields together and they share parameters, authorization, freshness, and invalidation.
- Load optional data and data guarded by create, update, approve, export, or other exact permissions only after the relevant permission passes and the user opens that workflow.
- After a typed mutation returns a resource, patch only the affected local state. Otherwise invalidate a named route dependency. Reserve `refreshAll()` for deliberately session-wide dependencies.
- A client read cache must name its owner, tenant and route-context key, maximum age, mutation and realtime invalidation events, and logout/user-change cleanup. Do not add a generic cache for auth, permissions, or sensitive user data.

### Permissions and routing

- `/api/auth/me` is the only current-user permission source. It populates the auth/permission stores.
- Use `$can.has`, `$can.hasAny`, `$can.hasAll`, `$can.hasModule`, and the route-access helper. Do not fetch a separate current-user permission list or inspect `authStore.user.permissions` directly.
- Staff/admin workspaces are read-first: module readers can enter and inspect; create/update/delete/approve/export controls and data load only after the exact permission passes.
- Never let a read-only page fail because it eagerly requests admin/action-only data.
- `_meta.menu` describes real sidebar menu records and may use a module/read permission for discovery.
- `_meta.access` guards child, detail, create, edit, approval, and workflow routes that must not become menu records.
- Authenticated users who lack route access go to `/403` with the requested path.
- Long-lived workflows that need deep links, browser history, or independent lazy loading should be separate routes, not in-memory tabs.

### Attendance workflow semantics

- Attendance has arrival, lesson and special rounds. Preserve these domain categories across the backend, generated contracts and UI; do not reintroduce a separate flag-ceremony category. The service owner is [school-attendance](backend-school/crates/school-attendance/src/services.rs), composed by [AttendanceWorkspace](frontend-school/src/lib/features/attendance/AttendanceWorkspace.svelte).
- Opening a round previews its authorized roster without creating per-person result records. Saving criteria or browsing a calendar does not preallocate rosters/results. Calendar highlights derive from the saved weekday rules and explicit date overrides; navigation alone must not persist a row for every displayed date.
- During a school day, save only explicitly checked results; an unselected person remains `unchecked` with no placeholder fact. After the next Bangkok midnight, the durable finalizer records `absent` for missing results in expected counted rounds using their date-effective roster. Skip cancelled and uncounted rounds, never infer `skipped`, and preserve explicit results. Finalization has an activation date and durable generation/date progress, catches up after downtime, and never runs as a side effect of browsing. Authorized teachers correct saved results through the revision/conflict and audit-reason flow. Clearing an unsaved selection is explicit.
- Arrival, lesson and special use the same writable statuses: `present`, `absent`, `late`, `skipped`, `sick_leave`, `personal_leave` and `activity`; `unchecked` is the draft/read state. Lesson and special show the canonical same-day arrival result as context without changing their draft. Score effects follow durable source changes. Closing a configured term requires finalization through its actual closing date and completed score delivery inside the lifecycle transaction.
- Per-person Attendance notes are absent from UI, API and persistence. Round correction/cancellation reasons, calendar notes and audit metadata have their own purpose and remain with their respective owners; this is not a ban on notes in other modules. Schema cleanup and Attendance-only tenant resets follow [the operational cutover](docs/OPERATIONS.md#attendance-sparse-result-cutover-and-scoped-reset), not page-rendering or routine-save side effects.

### Behavior score ownership

- Behavior owns manual behavior facts, immutable effective score rules, separate merit/deduction totals, score cycles and authorized cleanup. Attendance owns presence facts; application adapters deliver its durable changes without a dependency from either domain into the other.
- Use the existing student UUID and date-effective academic membership. Server rules determine protected points and authenticated creator ownership. Bulk selection deduplicates students, checks every member, and commits one replay-safe decision. Creator cancellation does not grant school-wide cleanup.
- Each contribution has one current or historical aggregate owner. Lifetime totals combine those owners once; net comparisons use the same date range for both sides. Reset starts a new zero-valued cycle while retaining old daily totals.
- Cleanup can delete real event details under an explicit preview/reason and retention scope while preserving selected aggregates, or remove the selected effects. Reconcile before destructive deletion, retain only minimal receipts/suppression metadata, and prevent old source/request replay from recreating deleted points. Closed history is immutable except for separately authorized audited adjustments. Retention cleanup of Attendance details must not cascade into Behavior scores.

### UI Design System

These rules own the shared UI standard. Theme values and component defaults remain executable sources of truth; link to their owners instead of maintaining a second value inventory or a separate design guide. Apply this standard when creating or changing UI, and migrate existing pages by coherent workflow rather than combining unrelated visual rewrites.

Choose controls and detail surfaces according to the task on each page. These principles apply across workflows; domain-specific presentation belongs with the feature's components and tests.

#### Design tokens and component ownership

- Persisted school appearance is owned by the generated School API contract and [frontend-school/src/lib/appearance/](frontend-school/src/lib/appearance/). Its same-origin `/school-appearance.css` stylesheet applies tenant colors, font and density before first paint; `app.html` owns the inline classic font/mode bootstrap so no script download can stall body parsing. Self-hosted font faces, the bounded font gate and an independent CSS watchdog keep initial rendering stable. The root runtime initializes from that applied CSS, prepares fonts before atomic updates, and revalidates only on explicit signals or throttled visibility changes, never per SPA route. Personal light/dark mode stays owned by `uiPreferences`; prototype storage is only an adapter.
- [frontend-school/src/routes/layout.css](frontend-school/src/routes/layout.css) owns fallback theme tokens, typography, radii and shared density consumption. Shared spacing defaults live in a lower cascade layer so explicit utility overrides still work. Use semantic utilities such as `bg-card`, `text-muted-foreground`, and `border-border`; do not hardcode brand or status colors independently in each route.
- [frontend-school/src/lib/appearance/model.ts](frontend-school/src/lib/appearance/model.ts) owns school color derivation. `primary` remains the readable accent for text, icons, links and focus; `primary-fill` preserves the exact selected brand color for default Button/Badge fills, paired with contrast-checked `primary-fill-foreground`, `primary-fill-hover` and `primary-fill-border`. Keep status colors independent and derive these roles centrally rather than darkening the selected fill or remapping all accent text. Keep the pre-appearance visual language: neutral near-white canvas and white cards, quiet neutral secondary/muted/hover surfaces, thin subtle input/divider borders and restrained shadows independently of the school palette. Mirror the default roles in the fallback stylesheet. Shared control owners distinguish selected states from transient hover through their semantic state, surface, text or subtle border; avoid heavy outlines and inset selection underlines. Preserve readable text, semantic status tones and visible keyboard focus.
- Shared primitives in [frontend-school/src/lib/components/ui/](frontend-school/src/lib/components/ui/) own button/input sizes, variants, borders, radii, and focus styles. Select an existing variant before adding page-local overrides; make a new reusable variant in the owning component when a repeated need warrants it.
- Keep text hierarchy consistent through `PageHeader`, shared form labels, and component defaults. Use the configured app font and Tailwind spacing scale; change app-wide density or typography at its shared owner rather than compensating with route-local font sizes, heights, or arbitrary spacing.
- Ordinary School UI text, including course codes, student/application/certificate identifiers, scores, counts and times, inherits the selected school font. Use `tabular-nums` when aligned digits help comparison. Reserve monospace for technical source/code, URLs, debug output and diagnostic identifiers; preserve explicit document/export fonts and mathematical typography. Do not remap the global monospace family or force a font on all descendants.
- Reuse a shared component for repeated filter bars, form sections, action groups, and status presentations when their visual and interaction requirements match. Keep domain data and permissions with the feature owner; do not build a universal page component with unrelated workflow flags.

#### Modern visual direction and color

- New and coherently redesigned pages should feel modern, calm, and deliberate: clear text hierarchy, generous but consistent spacing, aligned sections, subtle borders, and selective emphasis. Choose the page pattern first, then use color to clarify its summary, context, and primary action.
- Use restrained theme-based color in welcome, summary, and workflow-context surfaces: a low-opacity `primary` tint or soft gradient fading into `card`, a subtle tinted border, and accent text or icons. Keep tables, long forms, and dense information regions on readable neutral surfaces; not every card needs a gradient or a different color.
- Read the [staff welcome card](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/+page.svelte) and the context header in [AcademicYearTermEditor](frontend-school/src/lib/components/academic-core/AcademicYearTermEditor.svelte) as the approved visual references for this treatment. Reuse their hierarchy and restrained theme-token composition; their permissions, data flow, and workflow-specific content are not general templates.
- Keep primary actions visually prominent through the existing Button variant. Decorative accent colors must not compete with that action or change the established meaning of status, warning, error, or destructive controls. Reuse the same domain status mapping across controls, lists and summaries: `success` for successful outcomes, `warning` for warnings, `destructive` for errors/adverse outcomes and neutral treatment for unknown or unselected values. Domain categories keep their owning presentation. Use text or an icon as well as color, and make the selected state distinct from hover/focus.
- Compose colors from the owning theme tokens, including opacity modifiers and gradients. Additional category/chart colors need a real information purpose and the existing semantic/chart owner; do not scatter route-local hex values, independent palettes, or arbitrary accent choices across similar pages. Put a repeated new treatment or variant at the appropriate shared component owner.
- Check text, icons, borders, focus, and hover states on every tinted or gradient surface in light and dark themes and at mobile/desktop widths. Keep long content wrapping and card gaps intact; decoration must not clip content, obscure controls, or make a page's loading/error states harder to read.

#### Required School UI primitives

All new or coherently changed School standard controls use the local shadcn-svelte owners under `src/lib/components/ui/`, composed with app-layout/app-state where applicable. Do not recreate an ordinary control with route-local HTML/CSS, copy a styled control from another page, or introduce a second component library for the same responsibility. Native semantic layout elements and domain-specific interactive surfaces remain valid.

| UI need | Required owner and convention |
| --- | --- |
| Actions and navigation | [Button](frontend-school/src/lib/components/ui/button/index.ts); use its `href` support for button-styled navigation. Primary actions use `default`, cancel/secondary actions use `outline` or the established secondary variant, low-emphasis tools use `ghost`, and destructive actions use `destructive`. Pending mutations use [LoadingButton](frontend-school/src/lib/components/app-state/LoadingButton.svelte) or an equivalent composition of the same Button for a domain-specific pending label. |
| Form fields | Local `Label`, `Input`, `Textarea`, `Select`, `Checkbox`, `RadioGroup`, `ToggleGroup`, and `Switch` as appropriate to the value. Use [DatePicker](frontend-school/src/lib/components/ui/date-picker/DatePicker.svelte) for standard date selection; reuse a domain-specific time/editor control when its input semantics require it. |
| Content and results | Local `Card` for a meaningful content group, `Table` for tabular results, and `Badge` for compact status. Plain semantic sections/lists are appropriate without a card wrapper. Paginated views reuse their existing Button-based controls; do not import a Pagination or DataTable component that does not exist in this tree. |
| Focused forms and confirmations | Local `Dialog` for a focused form, `AlertDialog` for an irreversible decision, `Sheet` for an appropriate side workflow, and `Popover` for brief contextual content. Keep titles, descriptions, focus handling, and dismiss behavior supplied by the primitive. |
| Navigation and tools | Local `Tabs` for in-place regions with one workflow owner, `DropdownMenu` for grouped commands, and `Tooltip` for supplemental hints. Routes remain the owner of workflows needing history or deep links; tooltips do not replace accessible labels. |
| Loading and feedback | [App-state components](frontend-school/src/lib/components/app-state/) (`PageSkeleton`, `TableSkeleton`, `PageState`, `LoadingButton`), local `Skeleton`/`Alert`, and the existing `svelte-sonner` toast owner. Durable errors and required recovery actions remain visible in the affected region or form; a transient toast alone is insufficient. |
| Icons | The installed `@lucide/svelte` set, with the owning component's size convention and an accessible name for icon-only actions. Do not mix another icon family incidentally. |

- Import the actual exports from `#lib/components/ui/<component>/index.js` or the documented existing component path. Inspect the local implementation for its props, snippets, and exports; upstream examples or [registry configuration](frontend-school/components.json) may use older aliases and are not the application's runtime import contract.
- A domain component such as [CalendarMonthGrid](frontend-school/src/lib/components/calendar/CalendarMonthGrid.svelte) or [TimetableBoard](frontend-school/src/lib/components/academic/timetable/TimetableBoard.svelte) owns a specialized workspace; compose it with standard controls instead of forcing that workspace into an unrelated primitive. State its necessary interaction/layout exception in the working agreement.
- If a standard primitive is missing, first verify local exports and existing compositions. Follow the upstream adoption workflow below to add it once under the UI owner when the workflow requires it.
- frontend-admin currently has its own Tailwind controls and no local shadcn-svelte library. Preserve its local owners for scoped Admin work. Establishing shadcn there requires an explicit component-library migration with its own local owner, dependency/configuration updates, and affected workflow checks; neither claim it already exists nor import School internals to simulate it.

#### Adopting missing shadcn-svelte components

- When a School workflow needs a control or interaction that local components and existing compositions do not cover, consult the [official shadcn-svelte documentation](https://www.shadcn-svelte.com/) before implementing it. Read the relevant component examples, source, installation instructions, and required dependencies; choose the component or composed pattern that fits the workflow. A documentation example may be a recipe made from several primitives, not a separately importable component.
- Compare the upstream example with the owning application's actual manifests, lockfile, Svelte/Bits UI/Tailwind APIs, [registry configuration](frontend-school/components.json), and existing UI helpers. Use compatible source and dependencies; do not upgrade the project incidentally to make a copied example work or assume upstream aliases, props, snippets, events, and exports match this checkout.
- Add the required component source once under `frontend-school/src/lib/components/ui/`, then import its actual local exports from the existing UI owner. Compose examples from those primitives and keep workflow-specific data, permissions, and state with the domain owner. Review CLI-generated changes before integration; do not rerun initialization or overwrite customized primitives, global CSS, aliases, or configuration as part of adding a component. Keep any required dependency and lockfile changes scoped to the addition.
- Adapt generated or copied code to the project's `#lib` imports, helpers, installed icon family, theme tokens, typography, component variants, typed props, and Svelte lifecycle conventions. Resolve missing dependencies, unsupported APIs, and SSR/browser differences at their proper owner; preserve keyboard access, focus behavior, and the existing loading/error flow. Do not paste an upstream example directly into a page and leave incompatible imports or a second visual convention for later.
- Verify the component in its actual consumer workflow, including SSR and browser rendering where applicable, keyboard/focus behavior, narrow and desktop layouts, and light/dark themes. Run the affected checks from section 11 and the UI review above. A successful CLI addition or resolved import alone is not evidence that the component works; fix reported issues before treating the integration as complete.

#### Page patterns

Pages serving the same kind of task use the same visual order and action placement. Keep only regions that the workflow needs; domain-specific boards and dense editors may use their appropriate pattern while retaining the shared header, controls, and state conventions.

| Page type | Standard order and placement |
| --- | --- |
| List/search | Page header with the primary create action when permitted → compact search/filter surface → results table or list → pagination when needed. |
| Create/edit | Page header → clearly labeled form sections with a constrained inner width → a consistent trailing action group with cancel before save. |
| Detail | Page header with resource actions → summary/status → related detail sections or independently navigable workflows. |
| Dashboard | Page header → summary regions → detailed regions; each independent region follows the route-loading policy. |
| Board/timetable | Page header → context selectors and tools → the scrollable workspace, following the full-height layout rules below. |

Apply these details to the selected pattern; they describe new or coherently changed workflows, not a mandate to rewrite every existing page at once:

- **List/search:** keep creation in header actions with its exact permission; keep filters together and results below them. Apply sorting/filtering/pagination to the complete authorized result scope, not only the visible page. Reset the page/cursor when its filter context changes. Show pending/error/empty states in the result region; preserve usable results for a background refresh. Use tables for comparable fields and cards/lists when the task needs a compact item presentation. Read [SchoolFontLibrary](frontend-school/src/lib/components/school-fonts/SchoolFontLibrary.svelte) for feature-owned result grouping, targeted mutation patches, and guarded deletion; add search/paging only when the actual workflow requires them.
- **Person directories:** when users need both field comparison and recognition by face, provide directly selectable list/table and portrait-card views through the local single-choice control. Both views share the same result scope, ordering, identifying information and exact-permission actions. Use this choice where the workflow needs it. Read `/staff/manage` for the table and `/staff/manage?view=cards` for the portrait cards: the [route](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/manage/+page.svelte) owns the view control and navigation, while [StaffDirectoryResults](frontend-school/src/lib/components/staff/StaffDirectoryResults.svelte) owns the shared item presentation and actions.
- **Create/edit:** use a semantic form with an explicit submit action, grouped sections, associated labels, field-local errors, and a constrained inner form width. Keep cancel before save in the trailing action group, mark non-submit controls `type="button"`, prevent duplicate submission, and retain entered values on validation, network, or conflict errors. Separate saved data from the editable draft. Read [CertificateCampaignForm](frontend-school/src/lib/components/certificates/CertificateCampaignForm.svelte) for typed form inputs, lazy-option states, and the action group; do not copy certificate-specific rules into other forms.
- **Detail:** put identity/status and exact-permission actions before related information. Group read-only values with semantic `dl`/`dt`/`dd` inside shared Card sections; reserve form labels and inputs for editable fields. Keep visible, consistent gaps between cards in both stacked and column layouts, and wrap long names, contacts, and addresses. Load independently authorized related regions independently; basic resource reads must not depend on edit/approval-only requests. Keep back navigation tied to valid context, and give deep workflows their own routes. Read [student detail loader](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/students/%5Bid%5D/+page.ts) for separate profile/history ownership, without copying student or academic permissions to unrelated details.
- **Dashboard:** expose only real, authorized summaries with clear scope and destinations. Keep variable-height service/workspace cards at their natural content height. When the workflow calls for cards to fill vertical gaps, use responsive columns with unbroken cards and one column on mobile; the next card follows a shorter card in its column without waiting for the tallest card in a shared row. Retain meaningful source and keyboard order, and do not clip services or equalize card heights. Independent summaries and work regions follow the streaming/retry policy; never replace a failed count with zero or add placeholder metrics as if they were live. Read [staff home loader](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/+page.ts) and its page for the actual overview owner, and [personnel overview](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/manage/overview/+page.svelte) for unequal chart-card heights; preserve each shared region's existing lifecycle.
- **Fixed summary scope:** when the workflow fixes the dataset, show its scope briefly and omit a redundant selector. Counts, charts, planning regions and drilldown destinations must use that same scope, including refreshes and old links carrying another filter. The [personnel overview loader](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/manage/overview/+page.ts) fixes its scope to active staff accounts; disabled and other account statuses are excluded from that overview. This personnel rule does not remove status filters from management directories or impose active-only semantics on unrelated dashboards.
- **Calendar/board/timetable:** keep date/version/scope navigation and tools in stable positions and render the workspace immediately for the selected context. Reuse the feature's grid/board owner; permit readable overflow and contextual detail surfaces. Preserve usable content while independent reads refresh, distinguish view/edit/pending states, and restrict creation/movement/deletion separately. Inspect the owning [calendar page](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/calendar/+page.svelte) or timetable components for the relevant interaction, not as an all-purpose CRUD template.
- **Public pages:** use the public layout and bounded public API owner. Do not impose the authenticated `PageShell`, staff navigation, or permission stores on public pages; use the same theme and standard controls when appropriate and preserve their SSR/SEO requirements.

Before adding a route, name its pattern, primary action, data/permission/context owners, and loading/empty/error/pending states in the working agreement. If no existing pattern fits, explain the workflow need and define the smallest coherent extension at the shared owner rather than silently inventing a new visual system.

#### Ordinary tables and physical room labels

- Ordinary School tables use the shared [Table.Head](frontend-school/src/lib/components/ui/table/table-head.svelte) and [Table.Cell](frontend-school/src/lib/components/ui/table/table-cell.svelte) owners and lower-priority [layout CSS density defaults](frontend-school/src/routes/layout.css) for matching logical first/last column edge padding. Consumer spacing utilities can override those defaults for specialized layouts. Keep the executable defaults as the source of truth; header and body content align, internal spacing and vertical density remain consistent, and consumers do not patch ordinary edge spacing independently.
- Dense timetable, calendar and other specialized grids may own their necessary spacing exception. State that exception in the working agreement. Keep readable column widths and local horizontal overflow instead of squeezing table content or overflowing the page.
- Physical rooms are displayed and searched by their human-readable names throughout lists, pickers and related workflows. Retain room/building codes as technical stored metadata and relationships, without exposing them as ordinary labels or edit fields. Academic homeroom/class identity codes are a separate domain and retain their established meaning.
- The [facility workspace](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/facility/buildings/+page.svelte) owns building-to-room navigation and URL context; its result components own compact building/room presentation. Hidden legacy room English names and floors remain stored when ordinary room forms edit visible fields.

#### Selected School reference pages

These reviewed references guide the matching page responsibility. Inspect the current component, loader, and relevant tests before reuse; the applicability rules still govern each changed boundary. A reference's approval does not make all of its domain behavior appropriate for another feature.

| Reference route | Implementation and intended example |
| --- | --- |
| `/staff/school-fonts` | [SchoolFontLibrary](frontend-school/src/lib/components/school-fonts/SchoolFontLibrary.svelte): result grouping, permissions, and focused resource actions. |
| `/staff/certificates/new` | [CertificateCampaignForm](frontend-school/src/lib/components/certificates/CertificateCampaignForm.svelte): grouped creation form, option states, validation, and trailing actions. |
| `/staff` | [Staff home](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/+page.svelte): restrained welcome color, independent summaries, and natural-height workspace cards that fill vertical gaps. |
| `/staff/manage` and `/staff/manage?view=cards` | [Staff directory](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/manage/+page.svelte) and [StaffDirectoryResults](frontend-school/src/lib/components/staff/StaffDirectoryResults.svelte): switchable portrait table/cards, URL-preserved context, shared identifying information and permission-controlled actions. |
| `/staff/manage/overview` | [Personnel overview](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/manage/overview/+page.svelte) and its [loader](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/manage/overview/+page.ts): fixed active-account scope, consistent drilldowns and natural-height chart cards that fill vertical gaps. |
| `/staff/calendar` | [Calendar page](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/calendar/+page.svelte): date/context tools, calendar workspace, and independently authorized interactions. |
| `/staff/timetable` | [Timetable page](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/timetable/+page.svelte) and [TimetableBoard](frontend-school/src/lib/components/academic/timetable/TimetableBoard.svelte): context/version tools and a readable scheduling board. |
| `/staff/students/[id]` | [Student detail](frontend-school/src/routes/%28app%29/staff/students/%5Bid%5D/+page.svelte): identity/status summary, spaced read-only sections, wrapping values, and independently authorized history. |

#### Workflow controls and detail

- Keep the primary task easy to find and group related secondary tools in a clearly named menu when separate buttons crowd the header. Keep frequent actions readily accessible. A `+` menu is appropriate when there are multiple ways to create an item; choose a labeled create button when it makes the task clearer. Menu entries retain their own permission and disabled-state checks.
- When creating from a selected date, resource, or scope, use that context for valid form defaults where the workflow supports it. Keep defaults visible and editable where permitted; preserve the user's edits while the form is open or retrying. Shared create entry points must apply the same context and authorization rules.
- Use popovers for brief contextual details and dialogs for focused forms or decisions. Use dedicated routes when workflows need deep links, history, or independent navigation. Show enough separation between multiple items in a detail surface to make their boundaries clear.
- Keep repeated navigation controls in stable positions within each responsive layout. Variable-length titles, counts, and labels must not move the controls between successive uses; follow the route-loading policy to preserve the usable workspace during background reads.
- Frequent, reversible choices from a small fixed set should be directly selectable in one tap using the local single-choice `ToggleGroup` or `RadioGroup`, rather than requiring a menu to open before every choice. Preserve the distinction between editing a draft and saving it; destructive decisions still require their existing confirmation. Keep a selected value selected on a repeated tap unless the workflow explicitly allows toggling it off, and provide an explicit clear action where clearing is valid. Read [AttendanceResultToggle](frontend-school/src/lib/features/attendance/AttendanceResultToggle.svelte) for this interaction, not as a universal domain-status template.
- Long option lists use the existing searchable selection composition with a bounded popup. Filter by a meaningful parent scope before a dependent choice where this reduces ambiguity, such as room before lesson period. Preserve a still-valid selection; clear or replace an invalid dependent selection deterministically. Distinguish no options from no search matches and a failed options read.
- Option labels show each distinguishing fact once, in a consistent compact order. Keep short identifiers such as room, period and time together on one line when space permits; allow a long title to wrap or truncate with an accessible full label without pushing its identifier into a disconnected line. Avoid duplicate subject/context text and stacked cards for every option merely to fill a picker.

#### Interaction, states, and responsive behavior

- Use a consistent primary-action variant and action placement within each page pattern. Use destructive variants for destructive actions and consistent confirmation for irreversible actions. Permission-controlled actions continue to follow the exact capability checks above.
- Forms use visible associated labels, consistent required-field indicators, and field-local validation messages. Align labels, controls, helper text and add/remove actions consistently in repeated field groups; keep group-level help once and identify each repeated input accessibly. Preserve entered values after a failed save; make success and failure feedback actionable and consistent with the shared app-state components.
- Use [frontend-school/src/lib/components/app-state/](frontend-school/src/lib/components/app-state/) for loading, empty, error, and mutation states. Match skeleton structure to the pending region to reduce layout shifts, and follow the existing first-paint and background-refresh rules; distinguish an empty dataset from no matching filter results and from a failed read. A calendar or board that already communicates an empty range may keep its workspace visible without a redundant empty-state box. Add empty-state guidance when it explains the result or provides a useful next action.
- Use mobile-first responsive layouts with the project's Tailwind breakpoints. Keep headers, context, navigation and filters compact so users can reach the primary work without scrolling through repeated explanations or oversized context cards. Keep control order and accessible labels consistent as actions wrap or stack; dense tables and fixed-format workspaces scroll horizontally instead of shrinking below readable widths. In compact item presentations, prioritize the item name and move secondary metadata to accessible details when needed. Keep information required to distinguish items or make the current decision visible.
- Standard buttons, single-choice toggles, selects and date pickers use their shared size variants on every input type, including touch and hybrid devices. Favor compact controls and information density; do not impose a blanket minimum target size, enlarge controls according to pointer capability or viewport width, or turn ordinary choices into padded tiles. Keep explanatory copy with the surrounding form section.
- Verify actual pointer/touch interaction, computed geometry and keyboard access on narrow and desktop layouts. Shared dense grids such as Calendar align their cells with their actions so targets cannot overlap. Let labels and content determine natural size, wrap or reorganize controls where needed, and preserve visible focus and native scrolling.
- Bounded lists may scroll within the page on mobile, but ordinary inline scroll regions must allow native scrolling to continue to the outer page at their top and bottom. Use scroll containment only for an intentional isolated surface such as a modal. Avoid wheel/touch interception when native CSS supplies the behavior, and keep controls above and below the list reachable.
- Keep rendered long lists bounded with the feature's paging or windowing owner. Filtering, bulk actions and saving follow their declared authorized scope, not accidentally just the mounted rows. State whether a bulk action affects the visible page, filtered results or the whole scope when the distinction matters; preserve drafts across paging and filtering.
- Routine loading/autosave feedback uses the existing action control, stable feedback surface or toast owner, with accessible busy/live status as appropriate. Do not add idle implementation messages or growing/shrinking status paragraphs above the working area. Keep durable failures and their recovery actions visible in the affected form or region; preventing layout shifts must not hide errors.
- Format person names through the owning typed display-name helper/contract consistently across cards, pickers and tables. Thai honorifics attach to the given name, with the appropriate separator before the family name. Person directories default to sorting by given name, then family name and a stable identity tie-breaker, excluding the honorific, unless the workflow explicitly specifies another order. Apply that order to the complete authorized scope before pagination. Render context labels once, such as a field labeled academic year with a value that does not repeat that label.
- Person-directory portraits use the existing authorized file delivery owner, a bounded list projection of the portrait reference and the relevant person resource context. Reuse identifying fields already owned by the list contract; do not fetch each person's full profile just to render their name or photo. Provide a stable fallback for missing or failed images and preserve the owning file component's cancellation and resource cleanup.
- Independently editable items use item-specific pending state so one save does not block unrelated choices. Order or coalesce repeated writes to the same item, reconcile authoritative responses and reject stale results. A failure must not overwrite a newer draft or undo an unrelated successful edit; report recovery through the existing error owner.
- Preserve keyboard access, visible focus, accessible names for icon-only actions, and readable contrast in both themes. Communicate status and errors with text or icons as well as color.

#### Calendar, timetable and camera surfaces

- For binary day editing, make the calendar cell a clear, keyboard-accessible target and use its highlight to communicate selection/state, with a stable legend and accessible date/status label instead of repeating status captions in every cell. Keep event-detail activation separate from changing the day's value; pending changes follow the item-specific mutation rules above. Derive display state from the domain rules rather than writing database rows merely to render a month.
- Timetable entries, including activities and breaks, stretch consistently within their allocated cells using the board owner's layout. Preserve readable text, grouping and overflow; do not force all cards on unrelated dashboards to equal height or clip content to meet a timetable treatment.
- Self-facing camera previews mirror the display by default with truthful guidance; a different convention needs an explicit workflow reason and clear guidance. The face guide scales to the viewport with enough usable width. Keep preview transforms and detection overlays aligned, and keep captured evidence/model coordinates under the camera owner rather than changing stored image orientation just to mirror the preview. Read [FaceStation](frontend-school/src/lib/features/attendance/FaceStation.svelte) and [face-camera](frontend-school/src/lib/features/attendance/face-camera.ts) for the current owner; preserve camera/resource cleanup and permission/consent requirements.

#### UI review and verification

- Review changed UI against its page pattern and the shared token/component owners. Explain a necessary pattern exception in the change's review context, including the workflow need; do not silently introduce a new local visual convention.
- For visual or interaction changes, inspect the affected workflow on a narrow mobile viewport and a desktop viewport, including light/dark themes and relevant loading, empty, error, validation, and pending-action states. Use sanitized fixtures for any captured screenshots, and record what was actually inspected rather than claiming visual coverage from static checks.
- For dashboard card layouts, use unequal-height fixtures and inspect computed geometry to confirm that the next card follows the shorter card with the intended gap and no clipped content. For switchable directory views, verify the visible result actually changes, repeated selection stays selected, pointer/keyboard operation works, and filters, paging and view survive reload/history/detail-return navigation without redundant primary or profile reads. Include long names and missing portraits in the affected UI checks; retain section 11's impact-based scope.
- For repeated-choice controls and nested scrolling, verify real pointer/touch and keyboard behavior where applicable: one-tap selection, valid clearing, computed target sizes, draft retention, and native page scrolling at both list boundaries. Use synthetic fixtures and ensure viewing or scrolling does not trigger unintended writes. Camera changes also require the affected preview/overlay checks and the physical acceptance described by their feature owner.
- Run the applicable frontend and browser checks from section 11 and [docs/TESTING.md](docs/TESTING.md). Reuse the existing layout guards in [frontend-school/tests/static/frontend-layout-components.test.mjs](frontend-school/tests/static/frontend-layout-components.test.mjs); add a focused guard for a durable UI boundary only when meaningful coverage or repeated regression justifies it.

### Shared Page Layout UI

- App pages use `frontend-school/src/lib/components/app-layout/PageShell.svelte` or `PageHeader.svelte`.
- `PageShell` owns the browser title. App pages must not add a duplicate `<title>`; pass the visible page title to `PageShell`.
- `PageShell` owns standard page padding and spacing; `(app)/+layout.svelte` must not add page padding around it.
- Page-local `PageShell` classes must not add outer padding, margins, `container`, `mx-auto`, or `max-w-*`.
- PageShell content uses the full available app width; do not put `container`, `mx-auto`, or `max-w-*` in `contentClass`. Constrain a specific inner form/card/text wrapper instead.
- Compact filter surfaces use `rounded-xl border bg-card p-3 sm:p-4` without a shadow and with `gap-3` between controls. When using `Card.Root` for a filter-only surface, override its content-card spacing with `gap-0 py-0`; keep filter actions in the same responsive row when space allows. Any compact card made from only `Card.Content` must also use `gap-0 py-0` on the root when the content declares its own vertical padding, so root and content padding do not compound.
- For full-height boards, timelines, tabs, and split panes, pass height with `h-full min-h-0 flex` and scroll the actual content pane. Do not size inner workspaces with `100vh`.

### Sidebar Navigation IA

- Keep navigation organized as configurable management workspace → department/work section → service link. The same persisted hierarchy drives the personal home and Sidebar.
- Route path, route code, user type, and required permission are system-owned. Workspace/section placement, display label, icon, active state, and ordering are school-owned and must survive route synchronization and deployment.
- Navigation placement never grants access. `/api/menu/user` must continue to filter system-owned routes through the actor's effective module permissions.
- Expanded mode shows management-workspace headings, department/work-section headings, and service links.
- Collapsed mode becomes a management-workspace icon rail; each workspace opens a flyout containing its permission-filtered sections and service links.
- Show a section only when `/api/menu/user` leaves at least one permitted child.
- Do not duplicate routes by role merely to shape navigation.

## 8. Realtime and Events

- Socket connection/heartbeat state belongs to `school-academic-http::websockets::WebSocketManager`, composed once by the application. Timetable draft presence, reservations, commit exclusion, authorized projections and bounded replay belong to the single shared `school-academic-timetable::collaboration::TimetableCollaborationRuntime`; HTTP writes and draft sockets use that same instance.
- Authenticate and bind connection identity server-side. Do not trust client-supplied user/school identity after connection establishment.
- Realtime authentication and authorization fail closed: reject the handshake or close the connection when identity, tenant, active-user status, or required permission cannot be verified or is revoked.
- WebSocket clients implement ping/pong heartbeat (normally every 30 seconds), stale-connection cleanup, and bounded reconnect with backoff/jitter.
- SSE/WebSocket events signal that data changed; clients re-read authoritative HTTP state when the event contract does not carry a complete typed resource. Notification/work broadcast lag emits the payload-free `reconcile_required` SSE control event; the central client reconciles auth, inbox and affected work state on that signal and reconnection. Permission/session signal loss still fails closed.
- Draft collaboration membership is tenant UUID + term + timetable version + server connection ID. Cursor/lease/hover/renew operations are memory-only after authorized join; cache authorized visibility indices instead of rebuilding whole workspaces on pointer frames. Broadcast only viewer-authorized context/source/portrait references and recipient availability; private candidates and ownership tokens never enter peer events.
- Every timetable content/lifecycle write uses the domain's mandatory typed mutation context and commit permit. Revalidate the central session and accepted authorization generation after the draft gate; pin the complete canonical resource footprint through transaction outcome and publication, independently of lease TTL/disconnect. Accepted work retains its task and tenant pool lease despite HTTP cancellation. Hydrate resources and actual trigger-updated version revision before COMMIT; uncertain outcomes invalidate and reconcile without automatic write retries.
- Normal committed draft changes publish scoped typed deltas and advance every recipient's sequence, including empty visibility watermarks. Keep the bounded per-connection replay journal in memory, compare before/after scope for block and lookup removals, carry authoritative lookup order IDs, and fence cold recovery against unfinished version transactions. Upstream and permission changes invalidate retained caches even without connected members. Process restart changes epoch and requires an authoritative authorized snapshot; PostgreSQL remains the write authority and stores no interaction frames/reservations.
- When changing connection URLs, identity, event payloads, or proxy behavior, test direct backend access and the deployed reverse-proxy path.

## 9. Security, PDPA, and Logging

- Never store or log a national ID in plaintext.
- Encrypt readable `national_id` values app-side with AES-256-GCM through the canonical
  `backend-school/crates/school-crypto/src/lib.rs` APIs and `ENCRYPTION_KEY`.
- Store searchable `*_national_id_hash` values as keyed HMAC-SHA256 blind indexes using `BLIND_INDEX_KEY`.
- Keep both keys stable after data exists. Rotation requires a dedicated, reviewed re-encryption/reindex operation.
- Do not reintroduce PostgreSQL `pgcrypto` helpers or database-role encryption settings for application fields.
- Secrets come from environment variables or the deployment secret store. Never commit them, print them, or include them in test fixtures.
- School-session CSRF is the stable domain-separated HMAC of tenant UUID plus session UUID, never the rotating raw credential. Ordinary HTTP and realtime validation never extend human idle. Apart from login/session creation and explicit password changes (which retain their atomic idle refresh within the original absolute limit), only `POST /api/auth/activity`, after exact origin, CSRF and `expectedSessionId` validation, can renew ordinary idle at the five-minute persisted cadence or stage periodic rotation; absolute expiry never slides. Login and `/auth/me` carry authoritative `SessionState` from already loaded metadata. Session mismatch is a no-mutation 409 `session_changed` and must not clear a newly selected session cookie.
- Periodic rotation is acknowledged: activity stages one pending credential while the current credential remains valid; repeated current-credential activity deterministically resends that same pending credential. Derive it with the stable session HMAC key, versioned domain, fixed-width tenant/session IDs, raw current credential and canonical PostgreSQL-microsecond pending timestamp; persist only the digest/timestamp and verify the reproduced digest before resend. Pending has no independent short timeout. A valid pending request promotes under lock after required security checks, clears pending and starts the exact 60-second previous-credential grace and rotation clock. Password changes and revocation clear pending. Digest allocation checks and locks cover current, previous and pending columns. Only activity owns periodic replacement cookies; business responses and realtime never issue them.
- School session and permission caches are owned by one backend-school process. Authentication cache entries live at most 60 seconds and must respect idle/absolute expiry; realtime database validation is shared per tenant/session/user for at most five minutes, with local expiry checks on each heartbeat. Never cache raw credentials or previous/pending acceptance. Guarded maintenance publishes final metadata with invalidation fences; promotion evicts every cached credential for its session even when it sends no replacement cookie. Identity/permission changes and session revocations synchronously invalidate affected cached identity before returning or doing fallible follow-up work; in-flight reads must not refill invalidated state. Exactly one backend-school process on one VPS is the supported topology; do not add replicas or overlap live School processes.
- Hidden browser tabs pause SSE and timetable WebSocket after 60 seconds. Resume must reconnect and reconcile authoritative state, including missed permission changes, without resurrecting connections or state after logout/disposal.
- A remembered-session replacement cookie uses only the remaining absolute session lifetime; rotation must never extend that boundary.
- New bcrypt passwords must pass the shared non-truncating validator: 8–128 Unicode scalar values and at most 71 UTF-8 bytes.
- Validate and authorize file access. Keep private uploads out of public static roots and return only authorized downloads.
- Minimize sensitive data in API responses, analytics, errors, audit payloads, and realtime events.

## 10. Deployment Constraints

- Runtime configuration is environment-driven; do not bake environment URLs, credentials, keys, or tenant identifiers into images.
- `compose.local.yml` is the sole local Compose owner. Local runtime and disposable database tests use Docker. `podman-compose.yml` is the sole production Compose owner for rootless Podman; production service/network ownership is unchanged.
- `pipeline.yml` alone owns automatic PR/main planning, artifact preparation and production release. `merge.yml` owns team integration, `operations.yml` explicit operational tasks, and `maintenance.yml` retention and public API certificate renewal. Reusable implementations have no independent push/PR/dispatch writers.
- Executable policy separates verification, build and deployment scopes. Unknown paths expand verification rather than silently skipping it. Automatic deployment compares each component against its trusted accepted input baseline and includes queued changes; manual scope can add work but cannot omit required reconciliation.
- Artifact-producing commands belong to the backend/frontend build composite actions and application Dockerfiles/scripts. Hash those owners and compiled public configuration; orchestration and timing-only changes must not invalidate compiled artifacts. Component-specific deployment implementations reconcile their own owners, while shared maintenance, runtime and release controls reconcile every affected component. Report selection reasons without dropping unaccepted work.
- Local checks use the owning runners or focused commands selected under section 11; GitHub does not execute test suites. Run selected checks before marking a PR ready; early draft PRs and checkpoint pushes follow section 1. Critical API, permission, auth, tenant, migration and recovery checks remain required for their affected scope.
- Dependencies, compiled test targets and release artifacts have separate keys including toolchain, platform/profile, source and compiled public configuration. Main builds save shared release caches; developer machines retain their own Cargo/npm caches. Debug/test output cannot replace a release binary, and cache hits never count as verification success.
- Before production changes, the exact SHA and latest attempt must pass planning and every selected Pipeline build. Validate immutable image digests and frontend bundle manifests, reread accepted state under the shared production lock, and reject a stale candidate before maintenance.
- Reconcile prepared scope again under the production lock: remove only work now covered by trusted accepted baselines, retain explicit requested scope, and reject changed hashes or any newly required unprepared component. If no work remains, finish without entering maintenance or changing accepted state. Every downstream release and acceptance consumer uses this reconciled plan.
- Emit reconciled plans and raw artifact identities only from jobs without deployment secrets; private preflight jobs consume the public plan and expose no identity outputs. Validate plan transport before maintenance so a missing, redacted or malformed output cannot start a partial release.
- Every production deployment enters Maintenance Mode, including frontend-only, backend-only, full, School, Admin and provisioning. Finish checks/build first; keep public maintenance active until all selected components pass readiness, tenant asset/browser acceptance and authenticated proxy smoke. Failure keeps maintenance active; use the retained immutable artifacts/recovery manifests to retry or roll forward.
- Within the shared production lock, Admin frontend and School may run concurrently after selected Admin backend readiness. Keep School stage/backend/promotion ordering and the final acceptance join across all selected branches; a failed branch must never publish accepted baselines or open maintenance. Pin preparation and deployment consumers to the same runner OS for compatible dependency/browser caches.
- Private per-release smoke probe tokens are mode-0600 operational secrets, never logged or cached. They bypass only the maintenance response; backend authentication remains enforced. Backend-authenticated Admin `/internal/` calls remain available for control-plane readiness.
- Provisioning consumes the trusted accepted frontend artifact, validates its public configuration and correlates completion by exact request UUID, repository, main ref and workflow identity.
- Every GitHub Node setup reads the exact full version from the root `.node-version` owner. Planning, artifact preparation, deployment tooling and release preflight must use that same version because frontend input hashes include the full running Node version. Never use a floating major selector at these boundaries. Cloudflare Wrangler actions retain their Node 24 action runtime and the exact Wrangler CLI version from the owning application lockfile.
- Deployment jobs reuse a minimal dependency closure derived from the owning application's lockfile. Cache identity includes locked versions/integrities, extraction recipe, runner platform and full Node version; only main writes this namespace. Validate restored tooling and install that same locked closure on a miss. Artifact preparation may prime it from its completed locked install; deployment tooling must not run application build/prepare hooks or replace another session's dependency directory.
- Compiler summaries distinguish current BuildKit execution/reuse from recorded builder-layer Cargo timings, library cache counters and executable compilation/linking. Keep summaries bounded and publish complete timing evidence separately; missing metrics must be reported unavailable, never as a successful cache hit or zero work.
- Frontend Admin carries no internal-service credentials. It stages code in an inactive Worker version, promotes only the version ID from that upload's structured output and applies its apex route. Operator migration operations authenticate through backend-admin; never restore an unauthenticated Worker proxy or pre-deploy `secret bulk`.
- A catalogued owned-fixture cleanup is an explicit privileged owner job: resolve the fixed active tenant through backend-admin's ordinary metadata and migration-connection provider, reject pending directory mutations, and verify the exact database/owner role marker and verify-full CA. The owner credential stays on the backend host in memory or private tmpfs; never pass it through GitHub outputs, arguments, durable journals, supplied executables, or a new decryptor. Ordinary runtime authority remains DML-only; do not grant TEMP or owner rights to runtime for an operation.
- Every production mutation entry observes the canonical owned-cleanup interlock before its first host upload or provider mutation, including retention. GitHub cancellation does not clear durable intent or an orphan client. Only a validated no-dispatch abort may release a pre-intent reservation; after dispatch, never replay DML or fabricate an exit. A bound actual client exit zero plus exact SQL identity and fresh preserved readback may recover a missing commit receipt. Completion requires the original approved snapshot, durable receipt links, preserved counter floors across recovery, and at least 300 seconds with the same serving release/image/process. Keep this executor limited to its reviewed fixed input catalogue, not arbitrary SQL, tenant, path, or command inputs.

- The Admin portal content uses only the exact `<base-domain>/*` zone route over existing proxied apex DNS. An additional exact `www.<base-domain>/*` zone route returns a permanent HTTPS apex redirect before rendering or maintenance checks; it never serves portal content or permits a www API Origin. Preserve apex/wildcard DNS and tenant routes; never use a wildcard hostname for the central portal. Do not recreate `admin.<base-domain>` or permit its Origin. Under the production lock, retirement may detach only that exact custom domain after verifying the production Admin Worker's ownership, with confirmed absence before acceptance. Never delete a Worker or tenant DNS to retire this alias.
- If the Admin Worker is absent, its serialized release may first create a minimal 503
  bootstrap without application code, bindings, routes, workers.dev or preview access.
  Only a recognized missing-service response allows creation; authorization, transport
  and malformed-response failures stop release. Preserve existing Workers and continue
  to stage/promote the exact application version normally. This path never targets
  School Workers, tenant DNS or application databases.
- Explicit Admin Worker recreation belongs to `Operations` on main under the shared
  production lock. Prepare a frontend-admin-only Pipeline and require its successful
  build gate before maintenance/deletion; reject queued School/backend changes. Delete
  only the named Admin script without forcing dependent-resource removal, then let
  the already-prepared normal Pipeline own recreation and acceptance after lock release.
  Reapply only the exact apex content and www redirect routes without replacing DNS or restoring the retired alias.
- School Rust formatting, workspace/architecture, API export and selected database consumers have one verification owner and reuse compiled targets. A database suite uses one disposable Docker server with a fresh owned database per invocation; never point fixtures at a shared tenant or production database.
- Use the pinned local Rust toolchain without removing other developer toolchains. Offline exporter verification reuses the compiled contract binary under a clean runtime environment.
- Cached Playwright browser files must match the installed Playwright version, runner OS, and architecture. Always verify a real browser launch and install missing host dependencies before running the existing frontend acceptance or E2E gates, including on cache hits.
- API origins use publicly trusted certificates for exactly `school-api.<base-domain>` and `admin-api.<base-domain>`. The shared remote TLS owner uses the pinned rootless Certbot DNS-01 container for both installer bootstrap and Maintenance renewal. Keep ACME state private and durable; mount each mode-0600 DNS credential from the server user's runtime tmpfs only for that invocation. No independent host timer or stored bootstrap/deployment DNS token may bypass the production lock.
- Public certificate activation enters universal maintenance, validates the full chain, exact SANs, key pair and expiry, atomically promotes one exported generation, and verifies both APIs with system CA trust and authenticated academic smoke before reopening the same accepted application release. TLS operations never publish application baselines. Recurring renewal checks latest-main prepared gates and refuses queued runtime changes or a pending application release; documentation-only main changes do not invalidate the trusted accepted application identity.
- All API maintenance, release and certificate renewal checks use system CA trust; never fall back to private CA trust or restore a retired certificate-transition operation.
- Before DNS cutover, verify the selected target origin with both intended API hostnames and public system CA trust. Only then change the two exact API A records to DNS only; reject ambiguous records and drift, preserve unrelated frontend/management DNS, and retain a complete two-record rollback snapshot. Do not verify a still-public hostname that may reach the old origin.
- Containers bind application ports to `0.0.0.0`.
- `/health` is dependency-free process liveness and owns recurring container and external uptime-monitor healthchecks. `/ready` verifies dependencies and is reserved for deployment, smoke, and intentional readiness checks so routine probes remain dependency-free.
- Keep local Docker and production Podman service networking, reverse-proxy routes, CORS origins, cookies, and public backend URLs aligned.
- Declare backend service/container DNS aliases in production Compose before starting the process. Release network gates only validate attachment and both aliases against the current address with bounded DNS retries. Never disconnect/reconnect a running backend to repair aliases: changing its address can strand established database sockets. Missing or stale network state keeps maintenance active and requires controlled recreation through the release.
- Required backend-school runtime values include `SESSION_HMAC_KEY`, `ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `BLIND_INDEX_KEY`, `INTERNAL_API_SECRET`, `DEPLOY_KEY`, `BASE_DOMAIN`, and `TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS`; `SCHOOL_ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS` is an explicit local-development allowlist.
- Admin uses its own opaque, database-revocable sessions, exact apex Origin and CSRF checks.
  School uses independent opaque sessions under `SESSION_HMAC_KEY`; neither accepts legacy JWTs.
  Never import School authentication internals into Admin or restore pre-session binaries.
- The initial Admin operator is prepared by the normal serialized Pipeline from temporary
  `ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL` and `ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD` Actions secrets. Validate those inputs
  before maintenance or migration, preserve existing identities/passwords on retry, and never
  promote a customer. A pending operator with a live verification link permits initial release;
  all login/session authorization still requires verified email. Remove bootstrap secrets after
  verification; never store them in the server runtime environment or frontend.
- Internal calls send `X-Internal-Caller`; caller-specific secrets may use `INTERNAL_API_SECRET_<CALLER>` with controlled fallback during rotation.
- Treat permission/menu sync, tenant migrations, encryption keys, file storage, and data cutover as deployment concerns. Follow `docs/OPERATIONS.md`.
- Legacy runtime compatibility is not a default requirement. Any temporary bridge must be limited to the approved rollout or rollback window and removed at its predefined cleanup gate; it must not become a permanent alias, fallback, dual path, or parallel business model.
- Before consumer cutover, decide whether the prior application version can safely use the expanded schema. Record this decision, the compatible schema boundary, and the point after which old binaries are prohibited in the approved cutover design. If backward binary compatibility would retain a harmful legacy contract, use a tested roll-forward recovery instead of assuming an old binary can be restored.
- Cockpit management is host-managed, not part of Compose or the application Nginx proxy. It listens only on `127.0.0.1:9090` and is published as `https://server.<base-domain>` through a distinct Cloudflare Tunnel connector running on the target VPS.
- Do not open inbound TCP 9090. Keep `root` disallowed in Cockpit and log in as the `schoolorbit` account that owns the production rootless Podman namespace. Give that account password-backed `sudo` access for Cockpit's explicit Administrative access switch; do not create a `NOPASSWD` rule.
- Keep `podman.socket` enabled and active in the linger-enabled `schoolorbit` user manager, with its API at `/run/user/<schoolorbit-uid>/podman/podman.sock`, so Cockpit sees the production rootless namespace. A root-owned Podman socket or rootful container namespace is not a substitute.
- Production Compose services use `restart: always`, and the same linger-enabled user manager enables `podman-restart.service` for host boot recovery. Bootstrap only enables the boot unit; releases reconcile it after universal maintenance is confirmed, without starting/stopping containers through systemd during deployment. Maintenance proxy configuration persists across reboot; host startup never substitutes for release acceptance.
- The management hostname intentionally uses a publicly reachable Cockpit login without Cloudflare Access. Require a unique `SCHOOLORBIT_SERVER_PASSWORD` of at least 10 characters, monitor authentication, and never pass the password or Tunnel token in command arguments, logs, checkpoints, or Git.
- Store the Cockpit Tunnel token only in the root-owned mode-`0600` token file used by cloudflared `--token-file`. Retain the previous management Tunnel and CNAME snapshot until the rollback window is closed explicitly.

## 11. Verification Matrix

Choose the smallest defensible verification set from the changed behavior, direct consumers, shared boundaries and failure risk. Record the affected scope and selected commands before running them. File placement alone, such as one edit under `backend-school/`, does not require testing every module in that application. Reuse valid results under section 1; rerun only checks whose relevant inputs or environment changed. Always run:

```bash
git diff --check
git status --short
```

For permission-contract changes, from `frontend-school`:

```bash
npm run generate:permissions
npm run check:permissions
npm run test:permissions
```

For API-contract changes, from `frontend-school`:

```bash
npm run generate:api-contracts
npm run check:api-contracts
npm run test:api-contracts
```

### Test integrity and legitimate corrections

- When approved behavior is unchanged, including a refactor, fix the production cause of a regression instead of editing expected results to match broken output.
- Test edits are valid for an approved behavior/contract change, an evidenced faulty or brittle test, or an environment/fixture defect. Retain equivalent-or-stronger coverage. Correct justified implementation-detail checks to the observable contract; legitimate test fixes are allowed.
- The writer records the old and new requirement and why each expectation, fixture or snapshot change is valid, with a reproducer and before/after evidence where feasible. Include this rationale in the PR, including existing workflow guards changed because an approved policy intentionally changed.
- The Reviewer independently assesses test changes against requirements and evidence. QA checks actual outcomes and regression detection; a green check alone does not establish correctness.
- Never remove cases, weaken assertions, disable/skip required checks, blindly bless snapshots, add retries or narrow coverage merely to get green. Repair the cause or report a genuine blocker under the recovery policy.

### Selecting verification scope

- For a bounded backend rule or query change, compile the affected package and consumers and run focused service/database tests for its behavior and access scope. Add transport, transaction, concurrency or denied-path cases when those boundaries change. Do not run unrelated domain database suites merely because they share an application.
- For frontend presentation or workflow changes, check the owning Svelte/TypeScript application, lint/format the changed files, and run the affected helper/component/browser workflows and direct consumers. A frontend-only change does not require Rust or database tests unless it changes their contract or behavior.
- For API or permission contracts, run the corresponding generation/check/tests above and verify the affected producers, consumers and authorization paths. An unchanged contract does not need regeneration as a ritual.
- For documentation, `.rules`, or skill-instruction-only changes, run the documentation-policy check. Add runtime checks only when executable code, configuration or runtime behavior also changed; do not rerun completed runtime suites solely for prose edits.
- Broaden coverage when shared authentication/tenant/session boundaries, cross-domain behavior, migrations, dependencies, workspace composition, or verification/deployment infrastructure affect multiple owners, or when investigation cannot establish a bounded impact. Select all affected owners and critical cases. Failures require diagnosis and the relevant regression checks; narrowing scope must not hide a failed required check.

Use an owning runner when its suite matches the selected impact. Explicit owner scopes still run that owner's whole suite; use the documented focused commands in `docs/TESTING.md` when a narrower package, service, test filter or browser workflow provides the required coverage. For example, from the repository root:

```bash
./scripts/pipeline verify --scope documentation
./scripts/test_backend_school.sh --package school-staff personnel_overview -- --nocapture
```

For broader affected owners, use explicit scopes such as `./scripts/pipeline verify --scope contracts,frontend-school`. The conservative application-level planner remains available when its coverage fits the impact or the impact cannot be bounded:

```bash
./scripts/pipeline verify --scope auto
```

Use `--scope full` only for changes that require repository-wide coverage, unresolved repository-wide impact, or an explicitly requested full rehearsal. The runner owns frontend lint/type/static or unit checks, backend formatting/workspace/architecture/API and disposable database checks, and deployment ShellCheck/shfmt/Bats/Node guards/Compose/actionlint. Do not run a focused command and then repeat it inside a larger suite without an additional coverage need. Backend Admin SQLx checks must use the owned disposable Docker fixture, not a shared database. Test selection does not weaken production build, migration, maintenance, readiness or deployed acceptance gates.

When creating, editing, or analyzing `.svelte`, `.svelte.ts`, or `.svelte.js`, use the project Svelte tooling and resolve all reported issues before completion.

For school authentication, session, or realtime identity changes, also run the affected focused Rust and frontend auth/session tests, session Playwright discovery or execution with a dedicated disposable account, and deployed proxy smoke as described in `docs/TESTING.md`. The central owners already cover their baseline fixtures and contract checks; add only coverage required by the changed behavior.

The deployment owner renders proxy templates and checks direct-origin deployment behavior. Every owned check is required; do not replace unavailable checks with weaker substitutes.

Run `scripts/smoke_test.sh` when authentication, CORS, readiness, proxying, deployment, or cross-service behavior changes. Run Playwright from `frontend-school` when browser auth or user workflows change. Credentials must come from `SMOKE_*` or `E2E_*` environment variables and must never be committed.

For canonical replacement or cutover changes, use sanitized legacy-shaped fixtures and test successful mapping, preservation of identities/relationships/invariants, safe retry, every known unmappable state, stale or missing reconciliation, and refusal of both consumer cutover and destructive cleanup before evidence is complete. After cleanup, add guards preventing removed legacy contracts and runtime fallbacks from returning. External rehearsals use disposable or protected copies and emit only bounded aggregate evidence.

Static-test ownership:

- Backend architecture constraints belong in `backend-school/tests/static_architecture.rs` or a repository script.
- Frontend behavior and cross-stack contract constraints belong in `frontend-school/tests/static/`.
- Add a focused guard when a durable boundary has repeatedly regressed; do not write static tests for temporary counts, progress checkpoints, or prose history.
